// Gap articles, part 2: The Philippines (pre-colonial Philippines, baybayin, Indigenous peoples, Martial Law,
// Filipinos overseas, Tubbataha Reefs, Mount Apo, government and Constitution).
ADDART({id:"precol",t:"Pre-colonial Philippines",c:"Philippines",i:"⛵",w:"History of the Philippines (900–1565)",r:["baybayin","lapulapu","sulu"],
p:[["Before Spain","For tens of thousands of years before the Spanish arrived in 1521, people lived, farmed, traded and built communities across the islands."],["Barangays and kingdoms","Most people lived in villages called barangays led by datus, while larger trading states grew in places such as Tondo, Cebu, Butuan and Sulu."],["Connected to Asia","Islanders traded gold, pearls and forest goods with China, Southeast Asia and beyond."]],
l:"Long before the Spanish arrived, the Philippine islands were home to thriving communities. Early humans lived on Luzon at least 50,000 years ago, and later Austronesian seafarers brought rice farming, boats and languages. By the year 1000, chiefdoms and trading states were exchanging goods with China and the rest of Southeast Asia, writing in their own scripts and building a rich culture that still shapes Filipino life.",
s:[["The first people","The oldest human remains found in the Philippines come from Callao Cave in Cagayan, where scientists discovered bones of a small human species, Homo luzonensis, at least 50,000 years old. Remains from the Tabon Caves in Palawan show that modern humans lived there tens of thousands of years ago."],["Austronesian seafarers","Around 4,000 to 5,000 years ago, Austronesian-speaking peoples spread through the islands, bringing pottery, farming, domesticated animals and skilled boat building. Their languages are the ancestors of Tagalog, Cebuano, Ilocano and the other languages of the Philippines."],["The barangay","Most early Filipinos lived in barangays, communities of a few dozen to a few hundred families. The word comes from balangay, the plank boats in which early settlers traveled. Each barangay was led by a datu. Society had nobles, freemen and dependents who owed labor or service."],["Trading states","Larger communities grew into trading centers. Chinese records mention traders from Ma-i in 971 and envoys from Butuan in 1001. Tondo and Maynila on Manila Bay, the Rajahnate of Cebu and the Sultanate of Sulu traded with China, Brunei, Java and Malacca."]],
k:[["Earliest humans","At least 50,000 years ago, Callao Cave"],["Austronesian arrival","About 4,000–5,000 years ago"],["Oldest written document","Laguna Copperplate Inscription, 900 CE"],["Oldest boats found","Butuan balangays, from about 320 CE"],["Basic community","The barangay, led by a datu"]],
d:[["c. 50,000 years ago","Homo luzonensis lives in Callao Cave, Cagayan"],["c. 800 BCE","The Manunggul Jar is made in Palawan"],["c. 2500 BCE","The Khufu ship is built in Egypt"],["900 CE","The Laguna Copperplate Inscription is written"],["c. 1380","Islam reaches Sulu; a mosque is built on Simunul Island"]],
s2:[["The Laguna Copperplate","The Laguna Copperplate Inscription, found in 1989 near Lumban, Laguna, is the oldest known written document from the Philippines. Dated to 900 CE, it is written in Kawi script and records that a man named Namwaran and his family were freed from a debt. It mentions places such as Tondo and shows links with Java."],["The Manunggul Jar","The Manunggul Jar, a burial jar from a cave in Palawan made around 800 BCE, has a lid showing two figures rowing a boat, believed to carry a soul to the afterlife. It is a National Cultural Treasure and appears on the old 1,000-peso banknote. It shows early Filipinos’ beliefs and artistry."],["Gold","Early Filipinos were master goldsmiths. Hoards found in Surigao and Butuan include belts, chains, sashes and jewelry of remarkable skill made between the 900s and 1200s. The Golden Tara, a gold statue of a Hindu-Buddhist goddess found in Agusan in 1917, shows influences from India and Java."],["Beliefs","Most early Filipinos honored spirits called anito and diwata and their ancestors. Spiritual leaders called babaylan, often women, led rituals, healed the sick and kept community traditions. Islam spread to Sulu and Mindanao from the 1300s, and the Sultanates of Sulu and Maguindanao became powerful states."],["Life and culture","Early Filipinos grew rice and root crops, fished, hunted and wove cloth. Many Visayans covered their bodies with tattoos, which the Spanish called pintados, the painted ones. Epics, songs, riddles and proverbs were passed down orally, and many people could read and write in baybayin."],["Arrival of Europe","In 1521 Ferdinand Magellan’s expedition reached the islands, and Lapulapu of Mactan defeated him in battle. In 1565 Miguel López de Legazpi began the Spanish colonization of the islands, and in 1571 he took Maynila from Rajah Sulayman. The Spanish period that followed lasted more than 300 years."]],
sp:["The balangay boats","In 1976 archaeologists in Butuan, Agusan del Norte, began uncovering the remains of large wooden plank boats called balangay, the oldest dated to about 320 CE. Built without nails, with planks joined by wooden pegs and lashings, they could carry dozens of people across the seas. In 2009 a team sailed a replica balangay around Southeast Asia."],
g:[["Barangay","A village community led by a datu"],["Datu","A chief or ruler of a barangay"],["Babaylan","A spiritual leader and healer"],["Austronesian","A family of peoples and languages spread across the Pacific and Indian oceans"]],
f:["The Laguna Copperplate, from 900 CE, is the oldest known Philippine document.","The word barangay comes from balangay, a kind of boat.","Homo luzonensis lived in Cagayan at least 50,000 years ago."],
fun:"The word barangay comes from balangay, the boats in which early settlers sailed.",
tl:"Bago dumating ang mga Espanyol, may maunlad nang pamayanan sa kapuluan. Namuhay ang mga tao sa mga barangay na pinamumunuan ng datu, nakipagkalakalan sa Tsina at Timog-Silangang Asya, at sumulat gamit ang sariling mga titik gaya ng baybayin."});

ADDART({id:"baybayin",t:"Baybayin",c:"Languages",i:"✍️",w:"Baybayin",r:["phlang","precol","alphabet"],
p:[["A Philippine script","Baybayin is a writing system used in the Philippines before and during early Spanish rule."],["Syllables, not letters","Each basic symbol stands for a consonant followed by the sound a."],["Alive today","Baybayin appears on banknotes, logos and art, and related scripts are still used by Mangyan and Tagbanwa communities."]],
l:"Baybayin is a pre-colonial writing system of the Philippines, used especially by Tagalog speakers to write their language. It is an abugida, a script in which each symbol stands for a consonant with a built-in vowel. Spanish missionaries recorded that many Filipinos could read and write it. Though it fell out of everyday use, baybayin has become a proud symbol of Filipino heritage.",
s:[["How it works","Basic baybayin has 17 symbols: three vowels and 14 consonants, each consonant pronounced with the vowel a, such as ba, ka and da. A mark called a kudlit placed above changes the vowel to e or i, and one placed below changes it to o or u. Words are written as a string of syllables."],["The name","Baybayin comes from the Tagalog word baybay, meaning to spell. It is sometimes called alibata, a name invented in 1914 by Paul Versoza from the first letters of the Arabic alphabet; scholars prefer baybayin."],["Spanish records","Early Spanish writers were surprised to find that many Filipinos, both men and women, could read and write. The Doctrina Christiana, printed in Manila in 1593, one of the first books printed in the Philippines, contains prayers in Spanish and in Tagalog written in both baybayin and Latin letters."],["Decline","Under Spanish rule, the Latin alphabet gradually replaced baybayin for most purposes. By the 1700s it was rarely used in the lowlands. Because it was often written on bamboo and leaves, which decay quickly, few old examples survive."]],
k:[["Type","Abugida, a syllabic script"],["Basic symbols","17: 3 vowels, 14 consonants"],["Vowel marks","Kudlit"],["Early printed example","Doctrina Christiana, 1593"],["Related living scripts","Hanunó’o, Buhid, Tagbanwa, Pala’wan"]],
d:[["1593","The Doctrina Christiana is printed with Tagalog in baybayin"],["1620","Francisco López adds a cross-shaped kudlit to cancel the vowel sound"],["1914","The name alibata is coined"],["1999","Four Philippine scripts are added to UNESCO’s Memory of the World register"],["2010","Baybayin appears on the New Generation Philippine banknotes"]],
s2:[["Cousins in Southeast Asia","Baybayin belongs to a family of scripts descended from the Brahmi script of ancient India, which spread through Southeast Asia. Its relatives include old scripts of Java, Sulawesi and Sumatra. Other Philippine languages, such as Kapampangan, Ilocano and Visayan languages, had their own versions."],["Scripts still in use","The Hanunó’o and Buhid Mangyan of Mindoro and the Tagbanwa and Pala’wan of Palawan still use related scripts. The Hanunó’o carve poems called ambahan into bamboo tubes. In 1999 these four scripts were inscribed in UNESCO’s Memory of the World register as Philippine Paleographs."],["The López kudlit","Because baybayin could not show a consonant without a vowel, words such as bundok had to be written as bu-do. In 1620 the Spanish friar Francisco López introduced a cross-shaped kudlit to cancel the vowel. Many modern writers use it, though it was not part of the original script."],["Revival","Since the late 1900s, interest in baybayin has grown. Artists, designers and tattoo artists use it, and it appears in government seals and on Philippine banknotes, where the word Pilipino is written in baybayin. Schools and online courses teach it, and some bills in Congress have proposed making it a national writing system."],["Writing your name","To write a name in baybayin, break it into syllables. For example, Maria becomes ma-ri-ya, written with the symbols for ma, ra with a kudlit above for ri, and ya. Some sounds, such as f and v, had no symbols, so modern writers adapt the script or use the nearest sound."],["The Calatagan Pot","A clay pot found at Calatagan, Batangas, in 1958 has an inscription around its rim in a baybayin-like script. Its writing has not been fully deciphered, and scholars still debate its age and meaning. It is kept at the National Museum of the Philippines."]],
sp:["The ambahan","The Hanunó’o Mangyan of Mindoro write ambahan, poems of seven-syllable lines, on bamboo using a related script carved with a knife. Ambahan are recited or chanted to give advice, express love or teach. Young people learn the script so they can read and write ambahan, keeping one of the oldest Philippine writing traditions alive."],
g:[["Abugida","A script in which each symbol is a consonant with a built-in vowel"],["Kudlit","A mark that changes the vowel sound of a baybayin symbol"],["Script","A system of written symbols"],["Ambahan","A Mangyan poem often written on bamboo"]],
f:["Baybayin comes from the Tagalog word baybay, to spell.","The name alibata was only invented in 1914.","Philippine banknotes show the word Pilipino in baybayin."],
fun:"The word Pilipino is written in baybayin on Philippine banknotes.",
tl:"Ang baybayin ay katutubong sistema ng pagsulat sa Pilipinas bago at noong unang panahon ng mga Espanyol. Bawat simbolo ay katinig na may tunog na a, at binabago ng kudlit ang patinig nito."});

ADDART({id:"indig",t:"Indigenous Peoples of the Philippines",c:"Philippines",i:"🪶",w:"Indigenous peoples of the Philippines",r:["banaue","phbio","phlang"],
p:[["Many peoples","More than 100 Indigenous groups live in the Philippines, from the mountains of the Cordillera to the forests of Mindanao."],["Living traditions","They keep their own languages, beliefs, crafts, music and ways of caring for the land."],["Rights by law","The Indigenous Peoples’ Rights Act of 1997 recognizes their rights to their ancestral lands and cultures."]],
l:"Indigenous peoples are communities whose cultures and ways of life continued with little change through colonial times. The Philippines is home to more than 100 Indigenous groups, numbering an estimated 14 to 17 million people. From the rice-terrace builders of the Cordillera to the Lumad of Mindanao, they carry knowledge, arts and traditions thousands of years old.",
s:[["Who they are","Indigenous Filipinos include the Igorot peoples of the Cordillera, such as the Ifugao, Kalinga, Bontoc, Kankanaey and Ibaloi; the Aeta and other Negrito groups of Luzon and the Visayas; the Mangyan of Mindoro; the Tagbanwa, Pala’wan and Batak of Palawan; and the Lumad of Mindanao, including the Manobo, T’boli, Bagobo and Subanen."],["Land and life","Most Indigenous communities are closely tied to their ancestral lands, which provide food, medicine and sacred places. Many practice rice-terrace farming, shifting cultivation, hunting, fishing and gathering forest products, guided by customary laws and elders."],["Arts and crafts","Indigenous Filipinos are known for fine weaving, such as the T’boli t’nalak made from abaca fiber, Ifugao woodcarving, Kalinga tattooing, Maranao-style metalwork and beadwork. Their gongs, bamboo instruments, dances and epics are treasured parts of Philippine culture."],["The law","The Indigenous Peoples’ Rights Act of 1997 recognizes Indigenous peoples’ rights to their ancestral domains, self-governance, cultural integrity and free and prior informed consent before projects on their lands. It created the National Commission on Indigenous Peoples to protect these rights."]],
k:[["Groups","More than 100"],["Estimated population","About 14–17 million"],["Key law","Indigenous Peoples’ Rights Act, 1997"],["Government agency","National Commission on Indigenous Peoples"],["World Heritage","Rice Terraces of the Philippine Cordilleras, 1995"]],
d:[["1992","Law creates the GAMABA, the National Living Treasures Award"],["1995","The Ifugao rice terraces become a UNESCO World Heritage Site"],["1997","The Indigenous Peoples’ Rights Act becomes law"],["2001","The Hudhud chants of the Ifugao are recognized by UNESCO"],["2023","Kalinga tattoo artist Apo Whang-od appears on the cover of Vogue Philippines"]],
s2:[["The Igorot of the Cordillera","The peoples of the Cordillera mountains of northern Luzon resisted Spanish conquest for centuries. They built the famous rice terraces, kept their own laws and beliefs, and are known for their woven textiles, woodcarving and gong music. Igorot is a general name, and many prefer to be called by their specific group."],["The Aeta","The Aeta and related groups, such as the Agta and Ati, are among the earliest inhabitants of the Philippines. Many live in the mountains of Zambales, Pampanga and Tarlac. The 1991 eruption of Mount Pinatubo destroyed many Aeta villages and forced thousands to move."],["The Lumad","Lumad is a Cebuano word meaning native, used for the non-Muslim Indigenous peoples of Mindanao. There are about 18 Lumad groups. Many live in mountains rich in forests and minerals, and some communities have faced conflict, displacement and threats over their land."],["Living treasures","The Gawad sa Manlilikha ng Bayan, or National Living Treasures Award, honors traditional artists who keep crafts alive. Awardees include T’boli weaver Lang Dulay and Hanunó’o Mangyan poet Ginaw Bilog. Kalinga tattoo artist Apo Whang-od, whose hand-tapped tattoos draw visitors from around the world, has also been widely honored."],["The Hudhud","The Hudhud is a long chanted epic of the Ifugao, performed during rice harvests, funerals and wakes. A single performance can take several days. UNESCO recognized it as a Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity in 2001. Few young people now learn it, and schools of living tradition work to teach them."],["Challenges and change","Indigenous Filipinos often face poverty, poor access to schools and health care, and pressure on their lands from mining, logging, dams and plantations. At the same time, many young Indigenous people are reviving their languages and crafts, and Indigenous knowledge of forests and farming is valued in caring for the environment."]],
sp:["Apo Whang-od","Maria Oggay, known as Apo Whang-od, is a Kalinga tattoo artist from Buscalan who has tapped traditional tattoos by hand with a thorn and soot ink for about 90 years. Once given to warriors and women, these tattoos are now sought by visitors from around the world. In 2023, aged about 106, she appeared on the cover of Vogue Philippines."],
g:[["Indigenous","Belonging to the original peoples of a place"],["Ancestral domain","Land that a community has held since time immemorial"],["Lumad","A Cebuano word for the non-Muslim Indigenous peoples of Mindanao"],["T’nalak","A sacred abaca cloth woven by the T’boli"]],
f:["More than 100 Indigenous groups live in the Philippines.","The Hudhud chants can take days to perform.","Apo Whang-od has been tattooing for about 90 years."],
fun:"T’boli weavers say the patterns of t’nalak cloth come to them in their dreams.",
tl:"Mahigit 100 katutubong grupo ang naninirahan sa Pilipinas, gaya ng mga Igorot, Aeta, Mangyan at Lumad. Kinikilala ng Indigenous Peoples’ Rights Act ng 1997 ang kanilang karapatan sa lupang ninuno at kultura."});

ADDART({id:"martiallaw",t:"Martial Law in the Philippines",c:"Philippines",i:"⚖️",w:"Martial law under Ferdinand Marcos",r:["marcos","ninoy","edsa"],
p:[["Military rule","On 21 September 1972, President Ferdinand Marcos placed the Philippines under martial law."],["Rule by decree","Congress was closed, opponents were arrested and the press was controlled."],["Lasting debate","Martial law was formally lifted in 1981, but Marcos held power until the People Power Revolution of 1986."]],
l:"Martial law in the Philippines refers to the period of military rule declared by President Ferdinand Marcos in 1972. Marcos said it was needed to stop communist rebellion and disorder. During martial law, he ruled by decree, closed Congress, controlled the media and jailed opponents, and thousands of people suffered human rights abuses. It remains one of the most important and most debated chapters in Philippine history.",
s:[["The declaration","Proclamation No. 1081, dated 21 September 1972, placed the whole country under martial law; Marcos announced it on television on 23 September. Marcos, elected in 1965 and reelected in 1969, was nearing the end of the two terms allowed by the 1935 Constitution. He cited bombings, a reported ambush of his defense secretary and the growing communist insurgency."],["Closing democracy","Soldiers closed Congress and newspapers, radio and television stations. Opposition leaders, including Senator Benigno Ninoy Aquino Jr., journalists and student activists were arrested. A curfew was imposed, and public protests were banned. A new constitution, ratified in 1973 through citizens’ assemblies, kept Marcos in power."],["Human rights abuses","Many people were imprisoned without trial, tortured, killed or made to disappear. Historian Alfred McCoy, citing Amnesty International and other sources, estimated about 70,000 imprisonments, 34,000 cases of torture and 3,257 killings. A 2013 law later recognized more than 11,000 victims and their families for reparations."],["Lifting and after","Martial law was formally lifted on 17 January 1981, but Marcos kept the power to make laws by decree. After Ninoy Aquino was assassinated on his return from exile in August 1983, public anger grew. In February 1986, after a disputed snap election, the People Power Revolution forced Marcos into exile in Hawaii."]],
k:[["Declared","21 September 1972, Proclamation No. 1081"],["Lifted","17 January 1981"],["President","Ferdinand E. Marcos"],["Recognized victims","More than 11,000 (Republic Act 10368)"],["Ended by","People Power Revolution, February 1986"]],
d:[["1972","Martial law is declared"],["1973","A new constitution is ratified"],["1981","Martial law is formally lifted"],["1983","Ninoy Aquino is assassinated at Manila International Airport"],["2013","The Human Rights Victims Reparation and Recognition Act is passed"]],
s2:[["The economy","In the early years of martial law, the economy grew and the government built roads, bridges, power plants, hospitals and cultural centers, and expanded rice production through the Masagana 99 program. Later, heavy borrowing, falling export prices and favoritism toward businessmen close to the Marcos family led to a severe crisis. Foreign debt rose to about US$26 billion by 1986."],["Cronyism and ill-gotten wealth","Close allies of Marcos, known as cronies, were given control of major industries such as sugar, coconuts and media. After 1986 the Presidential Commission on Good Government was created to recover wealth said to have been taken from the nation, and courts have ordered the return of billions of pesos, including Swiss bank deposits."],["Resistance","Students, workers, farmers, church groups, journalists and politicians resisted martial law through protests, underground newspapers and elections. Many paid with their freedom or their lives. Their courage helped build the movement that led to the People Power Revolution in 1986."],["Remembering the victims","The Bantayog ng mga Bayani in Quezon City honors people who fought the dictatorship, with their names carved on a Wall of Remembrance. Republic Act 10368 of 2013 recognized the victims of human rights violations and provided reparations funded partly by recovered Marcos wealth."],["Continuing debate","Supporters of Marcos remember the period for its infrastructure, peace and order and national discipline. Critics, historians and victims emphasize the abuses, the loss of democracy, corruption and the debts left behind. The election of Marcos’s son, Ferdinand Marcos Jr., as president in 2022 renewed public debate about how the period is remembered and taught."],["Safeguards since 1986","The 1987 Constitution limits martial law to 60 days, requires the president to report to Congress within 48 hours and allows Congress to revoke it and the Supreme Court to review it. These rules were applied when martial law was declared in Mindanao in 2017 during the Battle of Marawi."]],
sp:["The radio that would not stop","When martial law closed most news outlets, the Catholic station Radio Veritas and underground newspapers such as Ang Pahayagang Malaya and the mimeographed sheets of student groups kept spreading news. In February 1986, Radio Veritas called people to EDSA to protect soldiers who had broken away from Marcos, helping start the People Power Revolution."],
g:[["Martial law","Military rule that replaces normal civilian government"],["Decree","An order with the force of law made by a ruler"],["Curfew","A rule requiring people to stay indoors at certain times"],["Reparations","Payments made to repair a wrong"]],
f:["Martial law was declared in 1972 and formally lifted in 1981.","More than 11,000 victims were recognized under a 2013 law.","The 1987 Constitution limits martial law to 60 days."],
fun:"The 1987 Constitution limits any declaration of martial law to 60 days unless Congress extends it.",
tl:"Idineklara ni Pangulong Ferdinand Marcos ang batas militar noong 21 Setyembre 1972. Isinara ang Kongreso, kinontrol ang media at ikinulong ang mga kritiko, at maraming biktima ng paglabag sa karapatang pantao hanggang sa EDSA noong 1986."});

ADDART({id:"ofw",t:"Filipinos Overseas",c:"Philippines",i:"✈️",w:"Overseas Filipinos",r:["phecon","labor","trade"],
p:[["A global people","About 10 million Filipinos live or work outside the Philippines."],["Overseas Filipino workers","Around 2 million work abroad at any one time as nurses, seafarers, caregivers, engineers and domestic workers."],["Remittances","Money sent home by Filipinos abroad supports millions of families and a large part of the economy."]],
l:"Filipinos are one of the world’s great migrant peoples. Millions live permanently in other countries, and about 2 million work abroad on contracts at any one time as overseas Filipino workers, or OFWs. The money they send home supports families, schools and businesses, while their absence also brings sacrifice. Filipinos abroad have shaped communities from the Gulf to North America.",
s:[["Where they go","The most common destinations for OFWs include Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates, Kuwait, Qatar, Hong Kong, Singapore and Taiwan. Many Filipinos have settled permanently in the United States, Canada, Australia, Japan and Europe. There are about 4 million people of Filipino heritage in the United States alone."],["What they do","Filipinos abroad work in hospitals, ships, homes, hotels, construction sites, offices and factories. The Philippines is one of the world’s largest sources of nurses and caregivers, and Filipino seafarers make up a large share of the crews on the world’s merchant ships."],["Remittances","Money sent home by overseas Filipinos reached about US$38 billion in 2024, about 8 percent of the country’s economy. Remittances pay for food, schooling, medical care and houses, and help keep the peso stable. December, when families prepare for Christmas, is usually the busiest month for remittances."],["A government policy","The government began organizing overseas employment in 1974 under the Labor Code, intending it as a temporary solution to unemployment. It grew into a permanent part of the economy. In 2021 Congress created the Department of Migrant Workers to protect and support OFWs."]],
k:[["Filipinos overseas","About 10 million"],["OFWs at any time","About 2 million"],["Remittances","About US$38 billion in 2024"],["Government department","Department of Migrant Workers, since 2021"],["Migrant Workers Day","7 June"]],
d:[["1974","The Labor Code launches the overseas employment program"],["1995","The execution of Flor Contemplacion in Singapore sparks national outrage"],["1995","The Migrant Workers and Overseas Filipinos Act is passed"],["2013","The Overseas Absentee Voting law is expanded to all citizens abroad"],["2021","The Department of Migrant Workers is created"]],
s2:[["Bagong bayani","Overseas workers are often called bagong bayani, new heroes, for the sacrifices they make for their families and country. Each December, airports hold special welcomes for returning OFWs. The government gives awards to outstanding migrant workers and their families."],["The balikbayan box","The balikbayan box is a large cardboard box filled with gifts, clothes, food and household goods that overseas Filipinos send home. Families often fill a box slowly over months. Shipping companies carry hundreds of thousands of balikbayan boxes to the Philippines every year, especially before Christmas."],["Families apart","Working abroad often means years away from spouses, children and parents. Many children grow up with one or both parents overseas, keeping in touch by video calls. Grandparents and relatives often help raise them. The social costs of migration are a frequent subject of Filipino films and teleseryes."],["Dangers and protection","Some OFWs, especially domestic workers, face abuse, unpaid wages, confiscated passports or illegal recruiters. The 1995 execution of Flor Contemplacion in Singapore led to the Migrant Workers Act. The government sets rules for recruitment agencies, signs agreements with host countries and keeps labor offices in many embassies."],["Seafarers","Filipino seafarers are among the most numerous in the world’s merchant fleets, working on cargo ships, tankers and cruise ships. Their skill in English and their training have made them sought after. Maritime schools across the Philippines train thousands of new seafarers each year."],["A global community","Filipino communities abroad build churches, start restaurants, hold fiestas and celebrate Independence Day with parades, such as the Philippine Independence Day Parade in New York. Overseas Filipinos can vote in national elections, and many return home to retire or invest."]],
sp:["Flor Contemplacion","Flor Contemplacion was a Filipina domestic worker in Singapore who was convicted of murder and executed in March 1995, despite appeals from the Philippine government and doubts raised about the case. Her death caused nationwide grief and anger, strained relations with Singapore and led Congress to pass the Migrant Workers and Overseas Filipinos Act that year."],
g:[["OFW","Overseas Filipino worker, employed abroad on contract"],["Remittance","Money sent home by someone working away"],["Balikbayan","A Filipino returning home from abroad"],["Seafarer","A person who works on a ship"]],
f:["About 10 million Filipinos live or work abroad.","Remittances reached about US$38 billion in 2024.","June 7 is Migrant Workers Day in the Philippines."],
fun:"Balikbayan boxes are often filled slowly over many months before being shipped home.",
tl:"Mga 10 milyong Pilipino ang naninirahan o nagtatrabaho sa ibang bansa, at mga 2 milyon ang OFW sa anumang panahon. Malaki ang naitutulong ng kanilang padala sa pamilya at ekonomiya, ngunit malaki rin ang sakripisyo ng pagkakalayo."});

ADDART({id:"tubbataha",t:"Tubbataha Reefs",c:"Philippines",i:"🐠",w:"Tubbataha Reef",r:["reef","palawan","seaturtle"],
p:[["A jewel of the Sulu Sea","Tubbataha Reefs Natural Park protects two huge coral atolls in the middle of the Sulu Sea."],["World Heritage","It was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1993."],["Teeming with life","Its waters hold hundreds of kinds of coral and fish, sharks, turtles and seabirds."]],
l:"Tubbataha Reefs Natural Park is a marine protected area in the middle of the Sulu Sea, about 150 kilometers southeast of Puerto Princesa, Palawan. Its two coral atolls and the Jessie Beazley Reef are among the healthiest and most diverse coral reefs in the world. A UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1993, Tubbataha is guarded by rangers all year round.",
s:[["Location","Tubbataha lies in the Sulu Sea and belongs to the municipality of Cagayancillo, Palawan. It is far from any large island, which helps keep it pristine. The park covers about 97,000 hectares of sea, including the North and South Atolls and Jessie Beazley Reef."],["Coral atolls","An atoll is a ring of coral reef surrounding a lagoon. Tubbataha’s atolls sit on top of ancient volcanic peaks under the sea. Their reef walls drop steeply into deep blue water, more than 100 meters down, making the park one of the world’s great diving sites."],["Marine life","Tubbataha is home to about 600 species of fish, about 360 species of coral, about half of all coral species known in the world, and many sharks, rays, dolphins and whales. Hawksbill and green sea turtles nest on its small islands, and thousands of seabirds breed on Bird Islet."],["Protection","Tubbataha became the Philippines’ first national marine park in 1988. Fishing and collecting are banned. Park rangers from the navy, coast guard and local government live at a ranger station on the reef year-round, guarding against illegal fishing. The Tubbataha Reefs Natural Park Act of 2010 strengthened its protection."]],
k:[["Location","Sulu Sea, Cagayancillo, Palawan"],["Area","About 97,000 hectares"],["Coral species","About 360"],["Fish species","About 600"],["World Heritage","Since 1993, extended in 2009"]],
d:[["1988","Tubbataha is declared the first national marine park in the Philippines"],["1993","UNESCO inscribes Tubbataha as a World Heritage Site"],["2009","The World Heritage Site is extended to include Jessie Beazley Reef"],["2010","The Tubbataha Reefs Natural Park Act becomes law"],["2013","The USS Guardian runs aground on the reef"]],
s2:[["Diving season","Tubbataha can only be visited by liveaboard dive boats from mid-March to mid-June, when the sea is calm. Divers come from around the world to see walls of coral, schools of jacks and barracuda, manta rays and reef sharks. Visitors pay a conservation fee that helps fund the rangers."],["Seabirds","Bird Islet and South Islet host large breeding colonies of seabirds, including brown boobies, sooty terns, noddies and the rare Christmas Island frigatebird, which visits outside its breeding season. Seabird numbers have grown since rats were removed from the islets."],["Groundings","In January 2013 a US Navy minesweeper, the USS Guardian, ran aground on Tubbataha, damaging about 2,345 square meters of reef. The ship had to be cut apart to remove it. The United States paid compensation, and the case led to stricter rules on ships entering the park."],["Healthy reefs","Scientists regularly survey Tubbataha and find it has some of the highest densities of fish in the Philippines. Its reefs supply fish larvae carried by currents to other reefs across the Sulu Sea, helping restock fishing grounds far away."],["Threats","Even remote reefs face threats from warming seas, which cause coral bleaching, plastic waste carried by currents and illegal fishing. The rangers’ long-term presence and strict protection make Tubbataha a model for marine conservation in Southeast Asia."],["Why it matters","Healthy coral reefs protect coasts from waves, provide food and income for millions of people and are home to a quarter of all ocean species. Tubbataha shows how strong protection can keep a reef thriving and benefit the wider sea."]],
sp:["The ranger station","Tubbataha’s rangers live in shifts of about two months at a time on a small station built on stilts above the reef, with only the sea, the coral and the seabirds around them. They patrol in boats day and night, check visiting vessels and record wildlife. Their dedication has helped keep Tubbataha one of the healthiest reefs on Earth."],
g:[["Atoll","A ring-shaped coral reef around a lagoon"],["Lagoon","A shallow body of water separated from the sea by a reef"],["Coral bleaching","When stressed corals lose the algae that give them color and food"],["Marine protected area","An area of sea where activities are limited to protect nature"]],
f:["Tubbataha has about half of the world’s known coral species.","Rangers guard Tubbataha all year round.","Tubbataha became a World Heritage Site in 1993."],
fun:"Tubbataha’s reefs are home to about half of all coral species known in the world.",
tl:"Ang Tubbataha Reefs Natural Park ay protektadong bahura sa gitna ng Dagat Sulu, sakop ng Palawan. Isa itong UNESCO World Heritage Site mula 1993 at tahanan ng daan-daang uri ng korales at isda."});

ADDART({id:"apo",t:"Mount Apo",c:"Philippines",i:"⛰️",w:"Mount Apo",r:["mindanao","eagle","volc"],
p:[["The highest peak","Mount Apo, at 2,954 meters, is the highest mountain in the Philippines."],["A sleeping volcano","It is a potentially active volcano with steaming vents near its summit."],["Grandfather of mountains","Apo means elder or grandfather, and the mountain is sacred to the peoples who live around it."]],
l:"Mount Apo is the highest mountain in the Philippines, rising 2,954 meters above sea level in Mindanao, on the border of Davao City, Davao del Sur and Cotabato. A large, potentially active volcano with sulfur vents near its summit, it is covered in rainforest and mossy forest that shelter the Philippine eagle and thousands of other species. Its name means elder or grandfather.",
s:[["The mountain","Mount Apo is a stratovolcano, built from layers of lava and ash. It has no recorded eruptions, but steam and sulfur gases still rise from vents called solfataras near the summit, and hot springs flow on its slopes. PHIVOLCS classifies it as potentially active."],["Forests and wildlife","As you climb, lowland rainforest gives way to cooler montane forest and then to mossy forest, where trees are draped in moss, ferns and orchids. The mountain is home to the Philippine eagle, tarsiers, deer and many birds, insects and plants found nowhere else, including the waling-waling orchid."],["Protected park","Mount Apo was made a national park in 1936, one of the oldest in the country. The Mount Apo Protected Area Act of 2004 strengthened its protection. It is also an ASEAN Heritage Park. Climbers must get permits and follow rules to protect its fragile environment."],["Sacred mountain","For the Bagobo, Manobo and other Indigenous peoples of the area, Mount Apo is a sacred place, home of spirits and ancestors. Some call it Sandawa. Indigenous communities help manage the park and serve as guides and porters for climbers."]],
k:[["Height","2,954 m"],["Location","Davao City, Davao del Sur and Cotabato, Mindanao"],["Type","Stratovolcano, potentially active"],["National park","Since 1936"],["Meaning of name","Elder or grandfather"]],
d:[["1859","A Spanish-led party makes the first recorded climb of Mount Apo"],["1936","Mount Apo National Park is established"],["1984","Mount Apo becomes an ASEAN Heritage Park"],["2004","The Mount Apo Protected Area Act becomes law"],["2016","A forest fire burns part of the summit area, and trails are closed for recovery"]],
s2:[["Climbing Apo","Climbing Mount Apo usually takes two to four days. Popular trails start from Kapatagan in Davao del Sur, Kidapawan in Cotabato and Santa Cruz. Climbers pass forests, rivers, boulder fields and steaming vents before reaching the summit, where on clear days they can see Davao Gulf and the surrounding mountains."],["The first climb","The first recorded climb of Mount Apo was in October 1859, by a party led by Don José Oyanguren, with Bagobo guides. Earlier attempts had failed because of the thick forest, steep slopes and sulfur fumes. Today thousands of climbers reach the summit each year."],["The Philippine eagle","The forests of Mount Apo are one of the strongholds of the Philippine eagle, the national bird. The Philippine Eagle Center in Davao City, near the mountain, breeds eagles and teaches visitors about protecting them and their forests."],["Geothermal power","Heat from deep inside the volcano is used to make electricity at geothermal power plants on Mount Apo’s slopes. Hot water and steam from underground turn turbines, supplying power to Mindanao without burning fuel."],["Fire and recovery","In March 2016, during a drought caused by El Niño, a forest fire burned a large area near the summit. The mountain was closed to climbers for many months so the forest could recover. Rangers and Indigenous volunteers helped fight the fire and replant trees."],["Durian and coffee","The fertile volcanic soil around Mount Apo grows fruits and crops for which Davao is famous, including durian, pomelo, cacao and coffee. Davao chocolate made from local cacao has won international awards."]],
sp:["Apo Sandawa","Many Indigenous peoples around the mountain call it Apo Sandawa. In their stories, the mountain is the dwelling of powerful spirits and the resting place of ancestors. Before climbing, some guides make offerings and ask permission. Their traditions remind climbers that Mount Apo is not only a challenge but a sacred home to be respected."],
g:[["Stratovolcano","A tall, cone-shaped volcano built from layers of lava and ash"],["Solfatara","A volcanic vent that releases steam and sulfur gases"],["Mossy forest","A cool, misty forest where trees are covered in moss"],["Geothermal","Using heat from inside the Earth"]],
f:["Mount Apo is the highest mountain in the Philippines.","Apo means elder or grandfather.","Steam and sulfur still rise from vents near its summit."],
fun:"Apo, the name of the country’s highest mountain, means elder or grandfather.",
tl:"Ang Bundok Apo ang pinakamataas na bundok sa Pilipinas sa taas na 2,954 metro, sa Mindanao. Isa itong bulkang maaaring aktibo, tahanan ng Philippine eagle, at sagrado sa mga katutubong naninirahan sa paligid nito."});

ADDART({id:"phgov",t:"Government of the Philippines",c:"Philippines",i:"🏛️",w:"Government of the Philippines",r:["independence","martiallaw","democ"],
p:[["A democratic republic","The Philippines is a democratic republic with a president elected by the people."],["Three branches","Power is divided among the executive, the legislature and the judiciary."],["The 1987 Constitution","The country’s highest law was ratified by the people in 1987, after the People Power Revolution."]],
l:"The Philippines is a democratic and republican state. Its government follows the 1987 Constitution, written after the People Power Revolution, which divides power among three branches: an executive led by the president, a two-chamber Congress that makes laws, and a judiciary headed by the Supreme Court. Local governments, from provinces down to barangays, run day-to-day affairs.",
s:[["The Constitution","The 1987 Constitution was ratified in a plebiscite on 2 February 1987. It has a preamble and 18 articles, including a Bill of Rights. It created safeguards against dictatorship, such as limits on martial law and a single six-year term for the president. Earlier constitutions were adopted in 1899, 1935 and 1973."],["The executive","The president is head of state and head of government and commands the armed forces. The president and vice president are elected separately by direct vote for six-year terms; the president cannot be reelected. The president appoints department secretaries, who form the Cabinet. As of 2026 the president is Ferdinand R. Marcos Jr., elected in 2022."],["Congress","Congress has two chambers. The Senate has 24 senators elected by the whole country for six-year terms, half every three years. The House of Representatives has more than 300 members, most elected by districts and up to 20 percent through the party-list system. Bills must pass both chambers and be signed by the president to become law."],["The judiciary","The Supreme Court, with a chief justice and 14 associate justices, is the highest court. It can strike down laws and actions that violate the Constitution. Justices are appointed by the president from lists prepared by the Judicial and Bar Council and serve until age 70."]],
k:[["Type","Democratic republic, presidential system"],["Constitution","1987"],["President’s term","6 years, no reelection"],["Senators","24"],["Supreme Court justices","15"]],
d:[["1899","The Malolos Constitution creates the First Philippine Republic"],["1935","The Commonwealth Constitution is adopted"],["1973","A new constitution is ratified under martial law"],["1987","The current constitution is ratified on 2 February"],["1991","The Local Government Code gives more power to local governments"]],
s2:[["Local government","The Philippines is divided into 82 provinces and about 150 cities, which contain more than 1,400 municipalities and about 42,000 barangays. Governors, mayors, councilors and barangay captains are elected every three years. The Local Government Code of 1991 gave local governments more powers and a share of national taxes."],["The barangay","The barangay is the smallest unit of government, led by a punong barangay, or barangay captain, and a council. Barangays keep the peace, settle small disputes through the Katarungang Pambarangay system, provide basic services and issue clearances. Young people aged 15 to 30 elect their own Sangguniang Kabataan, or youth council."],["Elections","Filipinos aged 18 and over can vote. National and local elections are held every three years in May, and the president is elected every six years. The Commission on Elections, or COMELEC, runs elections. Since 2010, votes have been counted by machines. Voter turnout is usually high."],["Checks and balances","Each branch can check the others. Congress can impeach the president and other high officials, and the Senate tries them. The Supreme Court can declare laws unconstitutional. Independent bodies, including the Commission on Audit, the Civil Service Commission, the Ombudsman and the Commission on Human Rights, watch over government."],["Autonomous region","The Bangsamoro Autonomous Region in Muslim Mindanao, created in 2019 after a peace agreement, has its own parliament and a large degree of self-government over local matters. The Constitution also provides for an autonomous region in the Cordillera, though it has not yet been established."],["Changing the Constitution","The Constitution can be amended by Congress acting as a constituent assembly, by a constitutional convention or by a people’s initiative, but any change must be approved by voters in a plebiscite. Proposals to change it, often called charter change or cha-cha, have been debated many times but none has succeeded since 1987."]],
sp:["The Bill of Rights","Article III of the 1987 Constitution, the Bill of Rights, protects freedoms such as speech, the press, religion and peaceful assembly, the right to due process, protection against unreasonable searches and the right of the accused to remain silent and to have a lawyer. It also bans torture and secret detention, lessons drawn from the martial law years."],
g:[["Republic","A state in which power rests with the people and their elected representatives"],["Plebiscite","A direct vote of the people on an important question"],["Impeachment","Charging a high official with wrongdoing in order to remove them"],["Party-list","A system that gives seats in the House to groups and sectors"]],
f:["The president serves a single six-year term.","The Senate has 24 members elected by the whole country.","The barangay is the smallest unit of government."],
fun:"Filipinos aged 15 to 30 elect their own barangay youth councils, the Sangguniang Kabataan.",
tl:"Ang Pilipinas ay demokratiko at republikanong estado na sumusunod sa Saligang Batas ng 1987. Hinati ang kapangyarihan sa tatlong sangay: ang ehekutibo na pinamumunuan ng pangulo, ang Kongreso at ang hudikatura."});

;
/* data/gaps3.js */
// Gap articles, part 3: Planet Earth (the continents, the Atlantic and Indian oceans, deserts, soil, climate change).
ADDART({id:"continents",t:"The Seven Continents",c:"Geography",i:"🗺️",w:"Continent",r:["tect","pacific","sea"],
p:[["Great landmasses","Continents are Earth’s largest landmasses: Asia, Africa, North America, South America, Antarctica, Europe and Australia."],["Moving slowly","The continents ride on tectonic plates and have drifted across the globe over hundreds of millions of years."],["Many peoples","Asia is the largest and most populous continent, and Africa has the most countries."]],
l:"Continents are the great landmasses that make up most of Earth’s dry land. In the model most used in English-speaking countries, there are seven: Asia, Africa, North America, South America, Antarctica, Europe and Australia, sometimes called Oceania with the Pacific islands. Together they cover about 29 percent of Earth’s surface, and their shapes have changed over millions of years as plates move.",
s:[["Asia","Asia is the largest continent, covering about 44.6 million square kilometers, nearly a third of the world’s land, and home to about 4.8 billion people, around 60 percent of humanity. It stretches from the Arctic to the tropics and from Türkiye to Japan and Indonesia, and includes Mount Everest, the world’s highest point, and the Dead Sea, the lowest point on land."],["Africa","Africa is the second-largest continent, about 30.4 million square kilometers, with 54 countries and about 1.5 billion people, the youngest population of any continent. It contains the Sahara, the largest hot desert, the Nile, one of the world’s longest rivers, and the savannas and rainforests of the tropics. Humans first evolved in Africa."],["The Americas","North America, about 24.7 million square kilometers, includes Canada, the United States, Mexico, Central America and the Caribbean. South America, about 17.8 million square kilometers, holds the Amazon rainforest and the Andes, the longest mountain range above sea level. The two continents are joined by the narrow Isthmus of Panama."],["Europe, Australia and Antarctica","Europe, about 10.2 million square kilometers, is separated from Asia by the Ural Mountains and the Caucasus by tradition rather than by sea. Australia, about 8.5 million square kilometers with its islands, is the smallest and flattest continent. Antarctica, about 14.2 million square kilometers, is almost entirely covered by ice and has no permanent residents."]],
k:[["Number","7 (in the common model)"],["Largest","Asia, about 44.6 million km²"],["Smallest","Australia, about 8.5 million km²"],["Most people","Asia, about 4.8 billion"],["Share of Earth’s surface","About 29 percent"]],
d:[["c. 335 million years ago","The supercontinent Pangaea begins to form"],["c. 200 million years ago","Pangaea starts to break apart"],["1912","Alfred Wegener proposes continental drift"],["1960s","Plate tectonics explains how continents move"],["2017","Scientists argue that Zealandia, mostly underwater, is a continent"]],
s2:[["How many continents?","Not everyone counts continents the same way. Many countries teach seven. Some combine North and South America into one American continent, giving six, and some also combine Europe and Asia into Eurasia, giving five. The five rings of the Olympic flag stand for five inhabited continents. Geographers agree that the boundaries are partly a matter of tradition."],["Highest peaks","Each continent has a highest summit, together called the Seven Summits: Everest in Asia at 8,849 meters, Aconcagua in South America, Denali in North America, Kilimanjaro in Africa, Elbrus in Europe, Vinson in Antarctica and either Kosciuszko on mainland Australia or Puncak Jaya in New Guinea. Climbing all seven is a famous mountaineering challenge."],["Continents on the move","About 300 million years ago, all the continents were joined in a supercontinent called Pangaea. It broke apart, and the continents slowly drifted to their present positions. The matching shapes of South America and Africa and identical fossils found on different continents were early clues. The continents are still moving a few centimeters each year."],["Islands and continents","Large islands such as Greenland, New Guinea and Borneo are not continents, though they sit on continental crust. Island nations are usually counted with the nearest continent, so Japan and Indonesia belong to Asia and Madagascar to Africa. New Zealand rises from Zealandia, a mostly drowned piece of continental crust that some scientists call the eighth continent."],["People and continents","Each continent has its own mix of peoples, languages, religions and histories, but continents are not the same as cultures. Asia alone includes Arab, Chinese, Indian, Malay and Russian cultures. Over the centuries, trade, migration and empires have linked the continents ever more closely."],["Antarctica, a continent for science","Antarctica belongs to no country. The Antarctic Treaty of 1959 set the continent aside for peace and science and banned military activity. Thousands of scientists live at research stations in summer, studying ice, climate and wildlife. Its ice holds about 70 percent of the world’s fresh water."]],
sp:["The continent that sank","Zealandia is a huge piece of continental crust about 4.9 million square kilometers in size, but about 94 percent of it lies underwater in the southwest Pacific. Only New Zealand and New Caledonia rise above the sea. In 2017 scientists argued it should be recognized as Earth’s eighth continent, and in 2023 they published the first complete geological map of it."],
g:[["Continent","One of Earth’s great landmasses"],["Supercontinent","A giant landmass made of several joined continents"],["Isthmus","A narrow strip of land joining two larger areas"],["Archipelago","A group of islands"]],
f:["Asia is home to about 60 percent of the world’s people.","Antarctica holds about 70 percent of the world’s fresh water.","Zealandia is a mostly underwater continent."],
fun:"About 60 percent of all people live in Asia.",
tl:"Ang mga kontinente ang pinakamalalaking lupain sa Daigdig: Asya, Aprika, Hilagang Amerika, Timog Amerika, Antarctica, Europa at Australia. Bahagi ng Asya, ang pinakamalaking kontinente, ang Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"atlantic",t:"The Atlantic Ocean",c:"Geography",i:"🌊",w:"Atlantic Ocean",r:["pacific","explor","tect"],
p:[["The second-largest ocean","The Atlantic Ocean separates the Americas from Europe and Africa."],["A growing ocean","It is widening by a few centimeters each year along the Mid-Atlantic Ridge."],["Ocean of history","The Atlantic was crossed by explorers, migrants and the slave trade, shaping the modern world."]],
l:"The Atlantic Ocean is the second-largest ocean on Earth, covering about a fifth of the planet’s surface. Stretching from the Arctic to the Southern Ocean between the Americas on the west and Europe and Africa on the east, it was the stage for the age of exploration, the transatlantic slave trade and mass migration. Down its middle runs the longest mountain range on Earth, mostly hidden underwater.",
s:[["Size and shape","The Atlantic covers about 106 million square kilometers, including its seas, and has an average depth of about 3,600 meters. It is S-shaped, following the coasts of the continents around it. Its deepest point, in the Puerto Rico Trench, is about 8,400 meters deep. Major seas include the Caribbean, the Gulf of Mexico, the Mediterranean and the North Sea."],["The Mid-Atlantic Ridge","Down the middle of the ocean runs the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, an underwater mountain range about 16,000 kilometers long. Here two plates are pulling apart, and molten rock rises to make new ocean floor, so the Atlantic grows wider by about 2.5 centimeters a year. Iceland sits on top of the ridge, where it rises above the sea."],["Currents","The Gulf Stream, a powerful warm current, flows from the Gulf of Mexico up the east coast of North America and across to Europe, keeping western Europe much milder than other places as far north. It is part of a great system of currents that moves heat around the planet and may be slowing as the climate warms."],["Hurricanes","Atlantic hurricanes form over warm waters off Africa and the Caribbean between June and November. The same kind of storm is called a typhoon in the western Pacific and a cyclone in the Indian Ocean. Hurricanes such as Katrina in 2005 have caused enormous damage along the coasts of the Caribbean and the United States."]],
k:[["Area","About 106 million km²"],["Average depth","About 3,600 m"],["Deepest point","Puerto Rico Trench, about 8,400 m"],["Widening","About 2.5 cm a year"],["Share of Earth’s surface","About 20 percent"]],
d:[["c. 1000","Norse sailors led by Leif Erikson reach North America"],["1492","Columbus crosses the Atlantic"],["1866","The first lasting telegraph cable across the Atlantic is completed"],["1912","The Titanic sinks after hitting an iceberg"],["1927","Charles Lindbergh makes the first solo nonstop flight across the Atlantic"]],
s2:[["The age of exploration","For centuries Europeans hugged the coasts. In the 1400s, Portuguese sailors explored down Africa’s Atlantic coast, and in 1492 Columbus crossed the ocean. Within a few decades ships linked Europe, Africa and the Americas, opening the routes that Magellan’s expedition later followed into the Pacific."],["The transatlantic slave trade","From the 1500s to the 1800s, European traders forcibly carried about 12.5 million enslaved Africans across the Atlantic to work on plantations in the Americas. Millions died on the voyage, known as the Middle Passage. The trade caused immense suffering and shaped the populations and cultures of the Americas. It was abolished during the 1800s."],["Migration","In the 1800s and early 1900s, tens of millions of Europeans crossed the Atlantic to start new lives in the Americas, especially the United States, Canada, Argentina and Brazil. Ocean liners carried them in about a week. Today airplanes cross the Atlantic in a few hours."],["The Titanic","The RMS Titanic, the largest ship of its time, struck an iceberg in the North Atlantic on its first voyage and sank on 15 April 1912. More than 1,500 people died, partly because there were not enough lifeboats. The disaster led to new safety rules at sea. Its wreck was found in 1985, about 3,800 meters down."],["The Sargasso Sea","The Sargasso Sea, in the North Atlantic, is the only sea with no coastline. It is bounded instead by ocean currents and filled with floating mats of brown sargassum seaweed. European and American eels travel thousands of kilometers to breed there, and young sea turtles shelter among the weed."],["Life in the Atlantic","The Atlantic is home to whales, dolphins, sharks, sea turtles and rich fisheries such as the Grand Banks off Canada, once among the most productive fishing grounds on Earth. Overfishing caused the collapse of the Grand Banks cod fishery in 1992, a warning about the limits of ocean resources."]],
sp:["The Bermuda Triangle","The Bermuda Triangle, an area between Florida, Bermuda and Puerto Rico, is famous for stories of ships and planes that vanished. Investigations have found that accidents there are no more common than in other busy stretches of ocean, given the heavy traffic, sudden storms and strong Gulf Stream. Scientists say there is no mystery."],
g:[["Ocean ridge","An underwater mountain range where new ocean floor forms"],["Current","A steady flow of water in one direction"],["Middle Passage","The voyage of enslaved Africans across the Atlantic"],["Trench","A long, deep valley in the ocean floor"]],
f:["The Atlantic widens by about 2.5 cm a year.","The Sargasso Sea has no coastline.","The Mid-Atlantic Ridge is about 16,000 km long."],
fun:"The Sargasso Sea, in the Atlantic, is the only sea with no coastline.",
tl:"Ang Karagatang Atlantiko ang ikalawang pinakamalaking karagatan, sa pagitan ng Amerika at ng Europa at Aprika. Lumalawak ito nang mga 2.5 sentimetro bawat taon, at mahalaga ito sa kasaysayan ng paggalugad at migrasyon."});

ADDART({id:"indocean",t:"The Indian Ocean",c:"Geography",i:"🌊",w:"Indian Ocean",r:["zhenghe","tsunami","pacific"],
p:[["The third-largest ocean","The Indian Ocean lies between Africa, Asia, Australia and the Southern Ocean."],["The warmest ocean","It is the warmest ocean on Earth, driving the great monsoons of South and Southeast Asia."],["A highway of trade","For thousands of years, ships have carried spices, cloth and ideas across its waters."]],
l:"The Indian Ocean is the third-largest ocean, bordered by Africa to the west, Asia to the north, Australia to the east and the Southern Ocean to the south. It is the warmest of the oceans and powers the monsoon winds that bring rain to more than a billion people. For millennia it has been a highway for traders, sailors and pilgrims linking Africa, Arabia, India, Southeast Asia and China.",
s:[["Size and depth","The Indian Ocean covers about 70 million square kilometers, about a fifth of the world’s ocean area. Its average depth is about 3,700 meters, and its deepest point, in the Java Trench south of Indonesia, is more than 7,000 meters deep. Its islands include Madagascar, Sri Lanka, the Maldives, Mauritius and the Seychelles."],["Monsoons","Because land heats and cools faster than water, winds over the Indian Ocean reverse with the seasons. In summer, moist winds blow from the ocean onto South Asia, bringing heavy monsoon rains that farms depend on. In winter, dry winds blow from the land to the sea. Sailors learned to ride these winds across the ocean."],["Ancient trade","Long before Europeans arrived, ships from Arabia, Persia, India, Southeast Asia and China crisscrossed the Indian Ocean, carrying spices, pepper, cotton cloth, gold, ivory and porcelain. Swahili cities on the East African coast, such as Kilwa and Mombasa, grew rich from this trade. Religions such as Islam and Buddhism spread along the same routes."],["Chokepoints","Several narrow passages control shipping into and out of the Indian Ocean: the Strait of Hormuz, through which much of the world’s oil passes, the Bab-el-Mandeb at the mouth of the Red Sea, and the Strait of Malacca between Malaysia and Indonesia, one of the busiest shipping lanes in the world, leading toward the South China Sea."]],
k:[["Area","About 70 million km²"],["Average depth","About 3,700 m"],["Deepest point","Java Trench, more than 7,000 m"],["Largest island","Madagascar"],["Famous for","Monsoons and ancient sea trade"]],
d:[["c. 100 CE","Greek merchants describe Indian Ocean trade in the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea"],["1405","Zheng He’s first treasure fleet sails"],["1498","Vasco da Gama reaches India by sailing around Africa"],["1869","The Suez Canal links the Mediterranean to the Indian Ocean"],["2004","The Indian Ocean tsunami strikes 14 countries"]],
s2:[["Vasco da Gama","In 1498 the Portuguese navigator Vasco da Gama sailed around the southern tip of Africa and reached Calicut in India, opening a sea route from Europe to Asia. Portugal soon built forts along the ocean’s coasts and tried to control the spice trade, the beginning of European empires in the region."],["The Suez Canal","The Suez Canal in Egypt, opened in 1869, joined the Mediterranean to the Red Sea and the Indian Ocean. Ships no longer had to sail around Africa, cutting the voyage from Europe to Asia by thousands of kilometers and speeding up trade, travel and the movement of ideas."],["The 2004 tsunami","On 26 December 2004, a huge earthquake off Sumatra caused a tsunami that swept across the Indian Ocean, killing about 230,000 people in 14 countries, from Indonesia and Thailand to Sri Lanka, India and Somalia. Afterward, a warning system was built so coasts can be alerted within minutes."],["A living fossil","In 1938 a museum curator in South Africa identified a strange fish caught off the coast: a coelacanth, a fish scientists thought had died out with the dinosaurs. Coelacanths still live in deep waters off the Comoros, East Africa and Indonesia, and are called living fossils."],["Islands in danger","The Maldives, a chain of about 1,200 coral islands, is the lowest country in the world, with most land less than 1.5 meters above sea level. Rising seas caused by climate change threaten its existence. Its people and leaders have become strong voices for climate action."],["Wildlife","The Indian Ocean is home to whale sharks, dugongs, sea turtles, manta rays and coral reefs. Madagascar and its neighbors have many unique animals, such as lemurs. Large areas of the ocean are fished heavily for tuna, and conservation groups work to protect its reefs and wildlife."]],
sp:["Riding the monsoon","Arab and Indian sailors timed their voyages to the monsoon winds. They sailed from Arabia and East Africa to India with the summer monsoon and returned with the winter monsoon. Their ships, called dhows, were built of planks sewn together with coconut fiber and had large triangular sails. Dhows still sail parts of the ocean today."],
g:[["Monsoon","A seasonal wind that brings heavy rain"],["Chokepoint","A narrow passage that controls movement through it"],["Dhow","A traditional sailing ship of the Indian Ocean"],["Coelacanth","An ancient kind of fish once thought extinct"]],
f:["The Indian Ocean is the warmest ocean on Earth.","The coelacanth was found alive in 1938.","The Maldives is the lowest country in the world."],
fun:"The coelacanth, a fish thought extinct for 66 million years, was found alive in 1938.",
tl:"Ang Karagatang Indian ang ikatlong pinakamalaking karagatan at ang pinakamainit. Pinagmumulan ito ng monsoon na nagdadala ng ulan sa Timog Asya, at naging daanan ng kalakalan sa loob ng libu-libong taon."});

ADDART({id:"desert",t:"Deserts",c:"Geography",i:"🏜️",w:"Desert",r:["sahara","climate","antarc"],
p:[["Dry lands","A desert is a place that gets very little rain, usually less than 250 millimeters a year."],["Hot and cold","Deserts can be scorching, like the Sahara, or freezing, like Antarctica."],["Life finds a way","Desert plants and animals have clever ways to save water and survive."]],
l:"Deserts are regions that receive very little precipitation, usually less than 250 millimeters a year. They cover about a third of Earth’s land surface, from the sandy Sahara to the icy plains of Antarctica. Though harsh, deserts are home to remarkable plants, animals and peoples who have adapted to life with little water.",
s:[["Why deserts form","Many deserts form in belts about 30 degrees north and south of the equator, where dry air sinks. Others lie in the rain shadow of mountains, which block moist winds, or deep inside continents, far from the sea. Cold ocean currents create coastal deserts such as the Atacama in Chile and the Namib in Africa."],["Hot deserts","The Sahara, covering about 9 million square kilometers across North Africa, is the largest hot desert. Others include the Arabian, Kalahari, Australian and Mojave deserts. Daytime temperatures can soar above 45 °C, but nights can be cold because dry air and clear skies let heat escape quickly."],["Cold deserts","Some deserts are cold. The Gobi in Mongolia and China has bitter winters, and Antarctica, which receives very little snowfall, is technically the largest desert on Earth, covering about 14 million square kilometers. The Arctic is also a cold desert."],["Not just sand","Only about a fifth of the world’s deserts are covered by sand dunes. Most are rocky plains, gravel, salt flats or mountains. Wind shapes rocks into strange forms and piles sand into dunes that can be hundreds of meters high and slowly move across the land."]],
k:[["Rainfall","Usually less than 250 mm a year"],["Share of land","About one-third"],["Largest hot desert","Sahara, about 9 million km²"],["Largest desert overall","Antarctica, about 14 million km²"],["Driest","Atacama Desert, Chile"]],
d:[["c. 5000 BCE","The green Sahara begins drying into desert"],["1913","A record air temperature of 56.7 °C is reported in Death Valley"],["1930s","The Dust Bowl strikes the American plains"],["1994","The UN Convention to Combat Desertification is adopted"],["2007","The Great Green Wall project across Africa is launched"]],
s2:[["Desert plants","Desert plants save water in many ways. Cacti, found naturally only in the Americas, store water in thick stems and have spines instead of leaves. Many plants have very long roots or spread roots wide to catch rain. Some seeds wait years in the soil, then sprout, flower and make new seeds within weeks after a rare rain."],["Desert animals","Many desert animals rest in burrows by day and come out at night. Camels can go for days without drinking and store fat in their humps. The fennec fox loses heat through its huge ears. Kangaroo rats get almost all their water from the seeds they eat, and some beetles collect water from fog on their backs."],["The driest place","Parts of the Atacama Desert in Chile are among the driest places on Earth; some weather stations there have never recorded rain. Its dry, clear skies make it one of the best places in the world for telescopes, and its landscape is so Mars-like that scientists test rovers there."],["The green Sahara","The Sahara has not always been a desert. Between about 11,000 and 5,000 years ago, it had lakes, grasslands and animals such as hippos and giraffes, and people painted them on rocks. Changes in Earth’s orbit shifted the monsoon rains south, and the Sahara slowly dried out."],["Desertification","Desertification is the spread of desert-like conditions into dry farmland and grassland, caused by droughts, overgrazing, cutting trees and poor farming. It threatens millions of people. In Africa, the Great Green Wall project aims to restore a belt of land across the Sahel, south of the Sahara."],["Dunes by the sea","Not all sand dunes are in deserts. Coastal dunes form wherever wind blows sand inland from beaches, even in rainy places. The Dune of Pilat in France is the tallest in Europe, about 100 meters high, and the dunes of Lençóis Maranhenses in Brazil fill with rainwater lagoons each year. Coastal dunes protect the land behind them from storms."]],
sp:["People of the desert","Peoples such as the Tuareg and Bedouin of the Sahara and Arabia, the San of the Kalahari and Aboriginal Australians have lived in deserts for thousands of years. They know where to find water, which plants can be eaten and how to travel by the stars. Many traditionally moved with their herds between water sources and grazing lands."],
g:[["Desert","A region with very little rainfall"],["Rain shadow","A dry area on the side of mountains away from the wind"],["Oasis","A place in a desert with water and plants"],["Desertification","The spread of desert conditions into drylands"]],
f:["Antarctica is the largest desert on Earth.","Only about a fifth of deserts are covered by sand dunes.","Some places in the Atacama have never recorded rain."],
fun:"Antarctica, covered in ice, is technically the largest desert on Earth.",
tl:"Ang disyerto ay lugar na napakakaunti ang ulan, kadalasang wala pang 250 milimetro bawat taon. Maaari itong mainit gaya ng Sahara o malamig gaya ng Antarctica, at may mga halaman at hayop na marunong mag-ipon ng tubig."});

ADDART({id:"soil",t:"Soil",c:"Geography",i:"🪱",w:"Soil",r:["agri","rice","fungi"],
p:[["The living skin of Earth","Soil is a mixture of minerals, organic matter, water, air and countless living things."],["Slow to form","It can take centuries to form just a few centimeters of soil."],["Food from the ground","About 95 percent of the food people eat is grown, directly or indirectly, in soil."]],
l:"Soil is the thin layer of loose material covering much of Earth’s land, made of broken-down rock, decaying plants and animals, water, air and billions of living organisms. It anchors plants, stores water, recycles nutrients and supports almost all the food we eat. Because it forms so slowly and is easily lost, soil is one of the planet’s most precious resources.",
s:[["What soil is made of","A typical soil is about half solid material, mostly tiny mineral particles from weathered rock with a small amount of organic matter, and half spaces filled with air and water. The mineral particles are sorted by size into sand, silt and clay. A soil with a good mix of all three, called loam, is ideal for most crops."],["How soil forms","Soil forms as rock is broken down by weather, water, ice, plant roots and chemical reactions, and as dead plants and animals decay into humus. This happens very slowly: it can take hundreds of years or more to make a few centimeters of soil. Climate, the underlying rock, slope, living things and time all shape a soil."],["Layers","If you dig a pit, you see layers called horizons. At the top is a layer of leaf litter and dark, rich topsoil full of humus and life. Below is the subsoil, lighter in color, where minerals washed down from above collect. Deeper still is broken parent rock, and below that, solid bedrock."],["Life in the soil","A single teaspoon of healthy soil can hold more microorganisms than there are people on Earth, including bacteria, fungi and tiny animals. Earthworms, ants and beetles mix the soil and create channels for air and water. Fungi link plant roots, and bacteria turn nitrogen from the air into forms plants can use."]],
k:[["Main ingredients","Minerals, organic matter, water, air"],["Particle types","Sand, silt and clay"],["Food grown in soil","About 95 percent"],["World Soil Day","5 December"],["Time to form","Centuries for a few centimeters"]],
d:[["1881","Charles Darwin publishes a book on earthworms and soil"],["1883","Vasily Dokuchaev founds the modern science of soil"],["1930s","The Dust Bowl blows away topsoil from the American plains"],["2014","The UN begins marking World Soil Day on 5 December"],["2015","The UN celebrates the International Year of Soils"]],
s2:[["Soil and farming","Farmers keep soil fertile by adding compost and manure, rotating crops, planting cover crops and using fertilizers. Rice paddies in Asia have been farmed for thousands of years because flooding and silt keep them fertile. Volcanic soils, rich in minerals, make the slopes of volcanoes such as Etna in Italy and those of Java excellent farmland."],["Erosion","Wind and water can wash or blow away topsoil much faster than it forms, especially where forests are cut or land is plowed on steep slopes. The Dust Bowl of the 1930s, when drought and poor farming turned the American plains into dust, showed how disastrous soil loss can be. Terraces, trees and ground cover help hold soil in place."],["Soil and climate","Soils store more carbon than all the world’s plants and the atmosphere combined. Healthy soils with plenty of organic matter keep carbon out of the air. Plowing, burning and draining wetlands release it as carbon dioxide. Farming methods that build up soil organic matter can help fight climate change."],["Kinds of soil","Soils differ around the world. Dark, fertile soils of grasslands are among the best for grain. Red and yellow tropical soils, colored by iron, are often low in nutrients because heavy rain washes them out. Peat soils in wetlands are made almost entirely of partly decayed plants."],["Darwin and the worms","Charles Darwin spent decades studying earthworms. In 1881 he published a book showing that worms slowly bury objects and ancient ruins by bringing soil to the surface in their castings, and that they turn over enormous amounts of earth. It became one of his best-selling books."],["Protecting soil","Because soil is so slow to form, it must be protected. Planting trees, avoiding overgrazing, composting food waste and reducing chemical use help keep soil healthy. Soil scientists around the world map soils to help farmers choose the right crops and fertilizers, and the United Nations marks World Soil Day on 5 December."]],
sp:["A handful of life","Scoop up a handful of rich garden soil and you hold billions of bacteria, kilometers of fungal threads, thousands of tiny animals and perhaps an earthworm or two. Together they break down dead leaves, recycle nutrients, build soil structure and help plants grow. Scientists believe much of the life in soil has not yet even been named."],
g:[["Humus","Dark, decayed plant and animal matter in soil"],["Loam","Soil with a balanced mix of sand, silt and clay"],["Erosion","Wearing away of soil or rock by wind and water"],["Horizon","A layer in the soil"]],
f:["A teaspoon of soil can hold more microbes than there are people on Earth.","About 95 percent of our food comes from soil.","Soils hold more carbon than all plants and the air combined."],
fun:"A teaspoon of healthy soil can contain more living things than there are people on Earth.",
tl:"Ang lupa ay halo ng mga mineral, nabubulok na halaman at hayop, tubig, hangin at napakaraming maliliit na nilalang. Napakabagal itong mabuo, at halos lahat ng ating pagkain ay nagmumula rito, kaya kailangan itong pangalagaan."});

ADDART({id:"climchange",t:"Climate Change",c:"Geography",i:"🌡️",w:"Climate change",r:["climate","renew","phtyph"],
p:[["A warming planet","Earth’s average temperature has risen by about 1.3 °C since the late 1800s."],["Human causes","Burning coal, oil and gas releases greenhouse gases that trap heat in the atmosphere."],["Effects everywhere","Rising seas, stronger heat waves and changing rainfall affect people and nature around the world."]],
l:"Climate change is the long-term shift in Earth’s climate, and today it mainly means the rapid warming caused by human activities since the Industrial Revolution. Burning fossil fuels and cutting forests have raised the amount of carbon dioxide in the air to its highest level in millions of years. The results include rising seas, melting ice, more extreme weather and threats to food, water and health, especially in the most vulnerable countries.",
s:[["The cause","Greenhouse gases such as carbon dioxide, methane and nitrous oxide trap heat in the atmosphere. Before the Industrial Revolution, the air held about 280 parts per million of carbon dioxide. By 2024 it held about 424 parts per million, mainly from burning coal, oil and gas for electricity, transport and industry, and from clearing forests."],["The evidence","Thermometers on land and sea, weather balloons and satellites all show warming. 2024 was the warmest year on record. Glaciers are shrinking worldwide, Arctic sea ice is thinning, ice sheets in Greenland and Antarctica are losing mass, and sea levels have risen by about 20 centimeters since 1900. The Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change has concluded that human influence is the main cause."],["Effects on people","Climate change brings more frequent and intense heat waves, heavier downpours in some places and longer droughts in others. Rising seas threaten coastal cities and farmland with flooding and salt water. Crop yields, fisheries, water supplies and health are affected, and the poorest communities, which produced the least pollution, are often hit hardest."],["Effects on nature","Warmer oceans bleach and kill coral reefs, and the oceans are becoming more acidic as they absorb carbon dioxide. Many plants and animals are moving toward the poles or up mountains to find cooler conditions. Species that cannot move or adapt quickly enough face extinction."]],
k:[["Warming since 1850–1900","About 1.3 °C"],["Carbon dioxide in 2024","About 424 parts per million"],["Pre-industrial carbon dioxide","About 280 parts per million"],["Sea level rise since 1900","About 20 cm"],["Main agreement","Paris Agreement, 2015"]],
d:[["1856","Eunice Foote shows that carbon dioxide traps heat"],["1896","Svante Arrhenius calculates that more carbon dioxide would warm Earth"],["1958","Charles Keeling begins measuring carbon dioxide at Mauna Loa"],["1988","The Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change is founded"],["2015","Nearly every country signs the Paris Agreement"]],
s2:[["Early discoveries","In 1856 the American scientist Eunice Foote showed that a glass jar of carbon dioxide heated up more in sunlight than one of ordinary air. In 1896 the Swedish chemist Svante Arrhenius calculated that doubling carbon dioxide would warm the planet by several degrees. Modern measurements began in 1958, when Charles Keeling started recording carbon dioxide at Mauna Loa in Hawaii."],["Countries at risk","Small island nations such as Tuvalu, Kiribati and the Maldives, low-lying countries such as Bangladesh and the Netherlands, and dry regions such as the Sahel are among the most vulnerable to climate change. Rising seas threaten coastal communities, warmer seas can strengthen tropical storms, and changing rainfall affects farming. Many of the countries at greatest risk produce a small share of the world’s greenhouse gases."],["Paris and beyond","In the Paris Agreement of 2015, countries agreed to keep warming well below 2 °C and to try to limit it to 1.5 °C. Each country sets its own targets for cutting emissions. In 2022 countries agreed to create a fund to help vulnerable nations deal with loss and damage from climate change."],["Solutions","Cutting emissions means replacing coal, oil and gas with renewable energy such as solar, wind, hydro and geothermal; using energy more efficiently; electrifying transport; protecting and restoring forests and mangroves; and changing farming. Adapting means building sea walls, planting mangroves, improving warning systems and growing crops that tolerate heat and floods."],["Youth and action","Young people around the world have demanded stronger climate action. In 2018 the Swedish student Greta Thunberg began striking from school outside her country’s parliament, inspiring millions to join climate strikes. Young activists from places such as Uganda, the Pacific islands and the Amazon have also campaigned to protect forests, oceans and vulnerable communities."],["What you can do","Individuals can help by saving electricity, walking, cycling or using public transport, reducing waste and food waste, planting trees, eating more plant-based foods and learning about climate change. Speaking up, voting and supporting climate policies also matter, because the biggest changes require action by governments and companies."]],
sp:["The Keeling Curve","Since 1958, scientists have measured carbon dioxide in the air at the Mauna Loa Observatory in Hawaii, far from cities. The graph of their measurements, called the Keeling Curve, zigzags each year as plants in the Northern Hemisphere grow and decay, but climbs steadily upward, from about 315 parts per million in 1958 to about 424 in 2024."],
g:[["Greenhouse gas","A gas that traps heat in the atmosphere"],["Fossil fuel","Coal, oil or gas formed from ancient living things"],["Emissions","Gases released into the air"],["Adaptation","Changing to cope with new conditions"]],
f:["2024 was the warmest year on record.","Carbon dioxide in the air has risen by about half since before the Industrial Revolution.","In 2022 countries agreed to set up a fund for climate loss and damage."],
fun:"Eunice Foote showed that carbon dioxide traps heat in 1856, more than a century before climate change became world news.",
tl:"Ang pagbabago ng klima ay ang pag-init ng mundo dahil sa mga gas na nagmumula sa pagsunog ng uling, langis at gas. Nagdudulot ito ng pagtaas ng dagat, mas matinding init at bagyo, at lubhang apektado ang Pilipinas."});

;
/* data/gaps4.js */
// Gap articles, part 4: History (India, Japan, Korea, the Byzantine Empire, the Crusades).
ADDART({id:"indus",t:"India Through the Ages",c:"History",i:"🛕",w:"Indus Valley Civilisation",r:["hindu","buddh","gandhi"],
p:[["An ancient civilization","India’s history begins with the Indus Valley Civilization, one of the world’s first urban cultures."],["Great empires","The Maurya, Gupta and Mughal empires ruled much of the subcontinent at different times."],["Gifts to the world","India gave the world Hinduism and Buddhism, the decimal number system with zero, chess and much more."]],
l:"India, on the vast South Asian subcontinent, has one of the longest continuous histories of any region. From the planned cities of the Indus Valley more than 4,500 years ago, through the empires of the Mauryas, Guptas and Mughals, to British rule and independence in 1947, its peoples created great religions, sciences, arts and ideas that spread across Asia and the world.",
s:[["The Indus Valley Civilization","Around 2600 BCE, cities such as Harappa and Mohenjo-daro flourished along the Indus River in what is now Pakistan and northwest India. They had straight streets laid out in grids, brick houses, drains and sewers more advanced than many later cities, and a Great Bath. Their people used a script that has never been deciphered. The civilization declined after about 1900 BCE."],["The Vedic age","From around 1500 BCE, the Vedas, sacred hymns in the Sanskrit language, were composed and passed down orally. They form the foundations of Hinduism. Society was organized into groups that later developed into the caste system. Great epics, the Mahabharata and the Ramayana, were composed over the following centuries."],["The Maurya Empire","Around 321 BCE, Chandragupta Maurya founded an empire that united most of India. His grandson Ashoka, horrified by the bloodshed of his war against Kalinga, turned to Buddhism and promoted nonviolence and tolerance. He carved his edicts on pillars and rocks across the empire and sent Buddhist missionaries abroad. His lion pillar is now India’s national emblem."],["The Gupta golden age","Under the Gupta Empire, from about 320 to 550 CE, India enjoyed a golden age of science, mathematics, literature and art. The mathematician Aryabhata calculated the value of pi and explained eclipses. Indian mathematicians developed the decimal place-value system with zero, which spread through the Arab world to Europe as Hindu-Arabic numerals."]],
k:[["Indus Valley cities","c. 2600–1900 BCE"],["Maurya Empire","c. 321–185 BCE"],["Gupta golden age","c. 320–550 CE"],["Mughal Empire","1526–1857"],["Independence","15 August 1947"]],
d:[["c. 2600 BCE","Harappa and Mohenjo-daro flourish"],["c. 261 BCE","Ashoka’s war in Kalinga leads him to Buddhism"],["499 CE","Aryabhata writes his great work on mathematics and astronomy"],["1526","Babur founds the Mughal Empire"],["1947","India and Pakistan become independent"]],
s2:[["Religions of India","Hinduism, Buddhism, Jainism and Sikhism all began in India. Buddhism, founded by Siddhartha Gautama around the 5th century BCE, spread to Sri Lanka, Southeast Asia, China, Korea and Japan. Islam arrived through traders and later conquerors, and India today has one of the world’s largest Muslim populations. India is also home to Christians, Parsis and many other faiths."],["The Mughal Empire","In 1526 Babur, a descendant of Genghis Khan and Timur, founded the Mughal Empire. His grandson Akbar ruled wisely and tolerantly, inviting scholars of many religions to debate. Shah Jahan built the Taj Mahal in Agra as a tomb for his wife Mumtaz Mahal, completed around 1653. The Mughals ruled much of India until the 1700s."],["British rule","The British East India Company gained control of Bengal after the Battle of Plassey in 1757 and expanded across India. After the great uprising of 1857, the British government took direct control, ruling India as the British Raj. Railways and universities were built, but India’s wealth was drained and famines killed millions."],["Independence and partition","Led by Mahatma Gandhi, Jawaharlal Nehru and others, the Indian National Congress campaigned for independence. On 15 August 1947, British India became independent but was divided into India and Pakistan. Partition led to terrible violence and the movement of more than 10 million people. Nehru became India’s first prime minister."],["India today","India is now the world’s most populous country, with more than 1.4 billion people, and the largest democracy. It has a fast-growing economy, a space program that landed near the Moon’s south pole in 2023, and a film industry, centered in Mumbai, that makes more films than any other in the world."],["India’s influence across Asia","Indian religions, writing and art spread across Asia through trade and pilgrims. Buddhism traveled to China, Korea, Japan and Southeast Asia, and Hindu-Buddhist kingdoms built Angkor Wat in Cambodia and Borobudur in Java. The Ramayana epic was retold across Southeast Asia, from Thailand’s Ramakien to the Maranao Maharadia Lawana, and many words in Thai, Malay and Tagalog come from Sanskrit."]],
sp:["The cities of the Indus","Mohenjo-daro, built around 2500 BCE, was home to perhaps 40,000 people. Its houses had bathrooms connected to covered drains along the streets, and a huge public pool, the Great Bath, may have been used for ritual washing. Its people traded with Mesopotamia and made seals carved with animals and their mysterious script, which no one has been able to read."],
g:[["Subcontinent","A large, distinct part of a continent, such as South Asia"],["Sanskrit","An ancient language of India"],["Edict","An official order or announcement"],["Partition","The division of British India into India and Pakistan"]],
f:["The Indus Valley script has never been deciphered.","The decimal system with zero was developed in India.","Many words in Thai, Malay and Tagalog come from Sanskrit."],
fun:"The Filipino word guro, teacher, comes from Sanskrit.",
tl:"Isa ang India sa may pinakamahabang kasaysayan sa mundo, mula sa mga lungsod ng Indus Valley hanggang sa mga imperyo ng Maurya, Gupta at Mughal. Dito nagmula ang Hinduismo, Budismo at ang sistemang decimal na may zero."});

ADDART({id:"japan",t:"Japan Through History",c:"History",i:"🏯",w:"History of Japan",r:["china","ww2","korea"],
p:[["An island nation","Japan is a chain of islands off the coast of East Asia with a long and distinctive history."],["Samurai and shoguns","For centuries Japan was ruled by military leaders called shoguns and their samurai warriors."],["Modern power","Japan rapidly modernized after 1868 and became one of the world’s largest economies after World War II."]],
l:"Japan’s history stretches from ancient hunter-gatherers who made some of the world’s oldest pottery to the high-tech nation of today. Over the centuries, emperors, court nobles, samurai warriors and shoguns shaped a culture of great refinement. After two centuries of near isolation, Japan modernized at astonishing speed, fought and lost a world war, and rebuilt itself into an economic powerhouse.",
s:[["Ancient Japan","The Jōmon people, hunter-gatherers who lived in Japan for thousands of years, made some of the oldest pottery in the world, more than 15,000 years ago. Around 300 BCE, the Yayoi culture brought rice farming and metalworking from the Asian mainland. By the 500s CE, Buddhism and Chinese writing had arrived from Korea and China."],["The imperial court","In 794 the capital moved to Heian-kyō, now Kyoto, beginning the Heian period, a golden age of court culture. Noblewomen wrote some of the first great works of Japanese literature, including The Tale of Genji by Murasaki Shikibu, written around 1000 CE and often called the world’s first novel."],["Samurai and shoguns","From 1192 military leaders called shoguns held real power, while emperors remained as symbols. Samurai, warriors who followed a code of loyalty and honor later called bushidō, served lords called daimyō. In 1274 and 1281 the Mongols tried to invade Japan, but storms, remembered as kamikaze, divine winds, helped destroy their fleets."],["The Tokugawa peace","After a century of civil war, Tokugawa Ieyasu became shogun in 1603, ruling from Edo, now Tokyo. The Tokugawa kept peace for more than 250 years. From the 1630s, they banned Christianity and closed Japan to most foreigners, allowing only limited trade with the Dutch and Chinese at Nagasaki. Arts such as kabuki, haiku and woodblock prints flourished."]],
k:[["Capital","Tokyo (Edo) since 1868"],["Tale of Genji","Written around 1000 CE"],["Tokugawa shogunate","1603–1868"],["Meiji Restoration","1868"],["Current era","Reiwa, since 2019"]],
d:[["794","The capital moves to Heian-kyō (Kyoto)"],["1603","Tokugawa Ieyasu becomes shogun"],["1853","US Commodore Matthew Perry’s ships arrive"],["1868","The Meiji Restoration begins Japan’s modernization"],["1964","Tokyo hosts the Olympics and the first bullet train opens"]],
s2:[["Japan and the outside world","In the 1500s and 1600s Japanese traders settled in ports across Southeast Asia, from Manila to Ayutthaya in Siam and Hội An in Vietnam. Portuguese merchants and missionaries brought guns and Christianity. In 1614 Japan banned Christianity, and from the 1630s the shoguns closed the country, allowing only limited trade with the Dutch and Chinese at Nagasaki."],["The Meiji Restoration","In 1853 American warships under Commodore Matthew Perry forced Japan to open to trade. In 1868 the Tokugawa shogunate fell and power was restored to Emperor Meiji. Japan quickly built railways, factories, schools, a modern army and navy and a constitution, transforming itself into an industrial power within a few decades."],["Empire and war","Japan defeated China in 1895 and Russia in 1905, and annexed Korea in 1910. In the 1930s it invaded China. In December 1941 it attacked Pearl Harbor and the American, British and Dutch colonies of Southeast Asia, occupying the Philippines, Malaya, Indonesia and Burma. The occupation caused immense suffering. Japan surrendered in August 1945 after the atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki."],["The economic miracle","After the war, Japan adopted a democratic constitution renouncing war and rebuilt its economy with remarkable speed. Companies such as Toyota, Sony and Honda became world famous. The 1964 Tokyo Olympics and the first shinkansen, or bullet train, showed the world a new Japan. By the late 1960s it had the world’s second-largest economy."],["Japan today","Japan is a constitutional monarchy with an emperor as a symbol of the state and an elected parliament. It is known for technology, anime, manga, video games and food such as sushi and ramen. It faces an aging, shrinking population and frequent natural disasters, including the 2011 earthquake and tsunami."],["Friends and partners","After the war, Japan paid reparations to several Asian countries it had occupied and went on to become one of the world’s largest givers of development aid, helping build roads, bridges, railways and ports across Asia, Africa and Latin America. Today it is a close partner of many countries in trade, investment, tourism and education."]],
sp:["The Tale of Genji","Written around 1000 CE by Murasaki Shikibu, a lady at the imperial court, The Tale of Genji tells the life and loves of Prince Genji in more than 50 chapters. Full of poetry and subtle feelings, it is one of the oldest novels in the world and remains a treasure of Japanese culture, inspiring paintings, plays and films for a thousand years."],
g:[["Shogun","A military ruler of Japan"],["Samurai","A member of Japan’s warrior class"],["Daimyō","A powerful lord in feudal Japan"],["Restoration","The return of power to a former ruler"]],
f:["Japanese Jōmon pottery is more than 15,000 years old.","The Tale of Genji is one of the world’s first novels.","Japan’s first bullet train opened in 1964."],
fun:"The Tale of Genji, written around 1000 CE by a woman at the Japanese court, is often called the world’s first novel.",
tl:"Mahaba ang kasaysayan ng Japan, mula sa sinaunang palayok, mga samurai at shogun, hanggang sa mabilis na modernisasyon matapos ang 1868. Matapos ang digmaan, naging isa ito sa pinakamalalaking ekonomiya at kaibigang bansa ng Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"korea",t:"Korea Through History",c:"History",i:"📜",w:"History of Korea",r:["koreanwar","japan","china"],
p:[["A peninsula people","Korea occupies a peninsula in East Asia between China and Japan."],["The Korean alphabet","King Sejong created hangul, one of the world’s most scientific alphabets, in the 1440s."],["Two Koreas","Since 1945 Korea has been divided into North Korea and South Korea."]],
l:"Korea, on a mountainous peninsula between China and Japan, has a history going back thousands of years. Its kingdoms developed a distinct culture, invented a remarkable alphabet and printed books with metal type before Europe did. In the 20th century, Korea suffered Japanese colonial rule, division and a devastating war, after which South Korea became a wealthy democracy whose music and dramas are loved around the world.",
s:[["The Three Kingdoms","From about the 1st century BCE, three kingdoms, Goguryeo, Baekje and Silla, competed for control of the peninsula. They adopted Buddhism and Chinese writing and built temples, tombs and palaces. In 668 CE, Silla, allied with China’s Tang dynasty, united most of the peninsula."],["Goryeo","The Goryeo dynasty, founded in 918, gave Korea its name in English. Goryeo craftsmen made beautiful blue-green celadon pottery. In 1377 Korean Buddhist monks printed the Jikji, the oldest surviving book printed with movable metal type, 78 years before Gutenberg’s Bible."],["Joseon","The Joseon dynasty ruled Korea for more than 500 years, from 1392 to 1897, with its capital at Seoul. It followed Confucian ideas of order, learning and respect for elders. Scholars took exams to enter government service. Joseon’s greatest king, Sejong, supported science and invented a new alphabet."],["Hangul","In 1443 King Sejong and his scholars created hangul, an alphabet designed so ordinary people could learn to read easily. Its letters are built from simple shapes that show how sounds are made in the mouth. Korea celebrates Hangul Day every 9 October. Today hangul is used in both North and South Korea."]],
k:[["Unified Silla","From 668 CE"],["Jikji printed","1377, with metal type"],["Joseon dynasty","1392–1897"],["Hangul created","1443"],["Divided","Since 1945"]],
d:[["668","Silla unites most of the Korean Peninsula"],["1377","The Jikji is printed with movable metal type"],["1443","King Sejong creates hangul"],["1910","Japan annexes Korea"],["1988","Seoul hosts the Summer Olympics"]],
s2:[["Admiral Yi and the turtle ships","When Japan invaded Korea in 1592, Admiral Yi Sun-sin defeated Japanese fleets with armored turtle ships, covered with iron spikes and armed with cannons. He won many battles despite being outnumbered and was killed in his final victory in 1598. He is one of Korea’s greatest heroes."],["Japanese rule","Japan annexed Korea in 1910 and ruled it as a colony until 1945. Koreans were forced to adopt Japanese names, and their language was suppressed in schools. On 1 March 1919, Koreans held nationwide peaceful protests for independence, the March First Movement, which was brutally crushed."],["Division and war","After Japan’s defeat in 1945, Korea was divided at the 38th parallel, with the Soviet Union occupying the north and the United States the south. In 1948 two separate states were created. In 1950 North Korea invaded the South, beginning the Korean War, which ended in an armistice in 1953 but no peace treaty."],["The Miracle on the Han","South Korea was one of the world’s poorest countries in the 1950s. Through hard work, education and export industries such as steel, ships, cars and electronics, it became one of the richest. Companies such as Samsung, Hyundai and LG are known worldwide. After years of military rule, South Korea became a democracy in 1987."],["North Korea","North Korea has been ruled since 1948 by the Kim family under a communist system that tightly controls its people. It is one of the world’s most isolated countries and has developed nuclear weapons, causing tension with its neighbors and the world. Many families separated by the division have never been reunited."],["The Korean Wave","Since the late 1990s, South Korean popular culture, called Hallyu or the Korean Wave, has spread around the world. K-pop groups such as BTS and Blackpink, K-dramas and films such as Parasite, which won the Oscar for Best Picture in 2020, have huge followings across Asia, the Americas and Europe. In 2024 Korean author Han Kang won the Nobel Prize in Literature."]],
sp:["King Sejong’s alphabet","King Sejong explained that he created hangul because ordinary people could not express their thoughts in difficult Chinese characters. He wrote that a wise person could learn it in a morning and even a slow learner in ten days. Its consonants are shaped like the mouth, tongue and throat making each sound, and its vowels are built from symbols for heaven, earth and humans."],
g:[["Peninsula","Land surrounded by water on three sides"],["Hangul","The Korean alphabet"],["Dynasty","A line of rulers from the same family"],["Armistice","An agreement to stop fighting"]],
f:["The Jikji was printed 78 years before Gutenberg’s Bible.","Hangul Day is celebrated every 9 October.","Parasite was the first non-English film to win the Oscar for Best Picture."],
fun:"The oldest surviving book printed with movable metal type, the Jikji, was printed in Korea in 1377.",
tl:"Mahaba ang kasaysayan ng Korea, mula sa mga sinaunang kaharian hanggang sa dinastiyang Joseon at sa paglikha ni Haring Sejong ng hangul. Mula 1945 nahati ito sa Hilaga at Timog Korea, at sikat ngayon sa mundo ang K-pop at K-drama."});

ADDART({id:"byzant",t:"The Byzantine Empire",c:"History",i:"⛪",w:"Byzantine Empire",r:["rome","ottoman","crusades"],
p:[["Rome in the East","The Byzantine Empire was the eastern half of the Roman Empire, which lasted for a thousand years after the West fell."],["Constantinople","Its capital, Constantinople, was the largest and richest city in Europe for centuries."],["Fall in 1453","The empire ended when the Ottoman Turks captured Constantinople in 1453."]],
l:"The Byzantine Empire was the continuation of the Roman Empire in the eastern Mediterranean, centered on its great capital, Constantinople. It lasted more than a thousand years, from the founding of the city in 330 CE until its capture by the Ottomans in 1453. Greek-speaking and Christian, it preserved ancient learning, built magnificent churches and shaped the Orthodox Christian world and the history of Europe and the Middle East.",
s:[["A new Rome","In 330 CE the emperor Constantine dedicated a new capital on the site of the old Greek city of Byzantium, on the strait linking Europe and Asia. He named it Constantinople. After the western empire collapsed in 476, the eastern empire continued. Its people called themselves Romans; the name Byzantine was given later by historians."],["Justinian and Theodora","The emperor Justinian, who ruled from 527 to 565, tried to reconquer the lost western lands and reclaimed Italy and North Africa. He collected Roman law into the Code of Justinian, which shaped legal systems in Europe and beyond. His wife, Empress Theodora, was a powerful adviser who helped save his throne during a revolt in 532."],["Hagia Sophia","Justinian built the great church of Hagia Sophia, Holy Wisdom, completed in 537. Its enormous dome seemed to float on light from 40 windows. For nearly a thousand years it was the largest church in the world. After 1453 it became a mosque, in 1934 a museum, and in 2020 it was turned back into a mosque."],["Faith and art","Byzantine Christians developed rich traditions of icons, sacred paintings of Christ and the saints, and glittering mosaics of gold glass. Disputes with the church in Rome grew, and in 1054 the Great Schism divided Christianity into the Eastern Orthodox and Roman Catholic churches. Byzantine missionaries brought Christianity and an alphabet to the Slavs of Eastern Europe."]],
k:[["Capital","Constantinople (now Istanbul)"],["Lasted","330–1453 CE"],["Language","Greek"],["Religion","Orthodox Christianity"],["Greatest church","Hagia Sophia, completed 537"]],
d:[["330","Constantine dedicates Constantinople"],["537","Hagia Sophia is completed"],["1054","The Great Schism divides Eastern and Western Christianity"],["1204","Crusaders sack Constantinople"],["1453","The Ottomans capture Constantinople"]],
s2:[["The walls of Constantinople","Constantinople was protected by the sea on three sides and by the massive Theodosian Walls on land, built in the 400s. For centuries these triple walls stopped armies of Persians, Avars, Arabs, Bulgars and Rus. Only cannon and a huge army finally broke them in 1453."],["Greek fire","The Byzantines defended their city with a secret weapon called Greek fire, a burning liquid that could be sprayed from ships and kept burning even on water. It helped destroy Arab fleets besieging Constantinople in the late 600s and early 700s. Its exact recipe was so closely guarded that it has been lost."],["Keepers of knowledge","Byzantine scholars copied and studied the works of ancient Greek writers such as Homer, Plato and Aristotle. When the empire declined, Greek scholars carried manuscripts to Italy, helping spark the Renaissance. Without Byzantium, many ancient texts might have been lost forever."],["The Fourth Crusade","In 1204, crusaders from Western Europe, who had set out to fight in the Holy Land, attacked and looted Constantinople instead, stealing treasures and dividing the empire. The Byzantines recovered their capital in 1261, but the empire never regained its strength."],["The fall of Constantinople","On 29 May 1453, after a siege of about 53 days, the Ottoman sultan Mehmed II captured Constantinople with a huge army and giant cannons. The last emperor, Constantine XI, died fighting. The city became the Ottoman capital, later called Istanbul. Many historians mark 1453 as the end of the Middle Ages."],["The Byzantine legacy","Byzantium’s influence lives on in the Orthodox churches of Greece, Russia, Serbia and other countries, in the Cyrillic alphabet, in Roman law and in art and architecture. The word byzantine is still used in English to describe something very complicated, after the empire’s elaborate court and bureaucracy."]],
sp:["Empress Theodora","Theodora rose from humble beginnings as a performer to become empress beside Justinian. In 532, when riots called the Nika revolt threatened the throne and Justinian prepared to flee, she reportedly declared that royalty makes a good burial shroud, urging him to stay and fight. He did, and kept his throne. She also championed laws protecting women."],
g:[["Icon","A sacred religious painting, especially in Orthodox Christianity"],["Mosaic","A picture made of small pieces of colored glass or stone"],["Schism","A split within a group, especially a church"],["Siege","Surrounding a city to force it to surrender"]],
f:["The Byzantines called themselves Romans.","Hagia Sophia was the world’s largest church for nearly a thousand years.","The recipe for Greek fire has been lost."],
fun:"The people we call Byzantines always called themselves Romans.",
tl:"Ang Imperyong Byzantine ang silangang bahagi ng Imperyong Romano na tumagal nang mahigit isang libong taon, mula 330 hanggang 1453. Ang kabisera nito, ang Constantinople, ay naging pinakamayamang lungsod sa Europa bago masakop ng mga Ottoman."});

ADDART({id:"crusades",t:"The Crusades",c:"History",i:"⚔️",w:"Crusades",r:["byzant","islam","christ"],
p:[["Holy wars","The Crusades were a series of religious wars launched by Christians of Western Europe from 1095."],["The Holy Land","The main goal was to capture Jerusalem and the lands around it from Muslim rulers."],["A lasting legacy","The Crusades brought great violence but also increased trade and contact between Europe and the Middle East."]],
l:"The Crusades were a series of religious wars fought mainly between Christians of Western Europe and Muslim powers in the Middle East from 1095 to the late 1200s. Crusaders sought to capture Jerusalem and the Holy Land, sacred to Christians, Jews and Muslims. The wars brought much bloodshed, including attacks on Jews and fellow Christians, but also increased trade and exchange of ideas between Europe and the Islamic world.",
s:[["The call","In 1095, at the Council of Clermont in France, Pope Urban II called on Christian knights to help the Byzantine Empire against the Seljuk Turks and to free Jerusalem. Those who went were promised forgiveness of their sins. Thousands sewed crosses onto their clothing; the word crusade comes from the Latin crux, cross."],["The First Crusade","Armies of knights and common people marched east in 1096. After great hardship and a long siege, the crusaders captured Jerusalem on 15 July 1099, killing many of its Muslim and Jewish inhabitants. They set up four crusader states along the eastern Mediterranean coast, including the Kingdom of Jerusalem."],["Saladin","In 1187 the Muslim sultan Saladin, ruler of Egypt and Syria, defeated the crusaders at the Battle of Hattin and recaptured Jerusalem. Unlike the crusaders in 1099, he allowed many Christians to leave the city safely. He became famous among both Muslims and Christians for his chivalry."],["The Third Crusade","Kings of England, France and the Holy Roman Empire led the Third Crusade from 1189 to 1192. Richard the Lionheart of England won battles but could not retake Jerusalem. He and Saladin agreed to a truce that allowed Christian pilgrims to visit the city."]],
k:[["Period","1095 to the late 1200s"],["Main goal","Control of Jerusalem and the Holy Land"],["First Crusade","1096–1099"],["Jerusalem retaken by Saladin","1187"],["Last crusader city lost","Acre, 1291"]],
d:[["1095","Pope Urban II calls for a crusade"],["1099","Crusaders capture Jerusalem"],["1187","Saladin recaptures Jerusalem"],["1204","The Fourth Crusade sacks Constantinople"],["1291","Acre falls, ending the crusader states"]],
s2:[["Military orders","Orders of warrior monks formed to protect pilgrims and fight in the Holy Land. The Knights Templar, founded around 1119, became wealthy bankers before being destroyed by the French king in the early 1300s. The Knights Hospitaller cared for the sick and later ruled Rhodes and Malta."],["Victims at home","Crusading armies also attacked people in Europe. In 1096, crusaders massacred Jewish communities in towns along the Rhine in Germany. Later crusades were directed against people the Church considered heretics in southern France and against non-Christian peoples around the Baltic Sea."],["The Fourth Crusade","The Fourth Crusade never reached the Holy Land. In 1204, its leaders, deep in debt to Venice, attacked the Christian city of Constantinople, looting its churches and treasures. The sack deepened the split between Eastern and Western Christians and weakened the Byzantine Empire."],["The end of the crusader states","Over the 1200s, Muslim rulers, especially the Mamluks of Egypt under Sultan Baybars, captured crusader castles one by one. In 1291, Acre, the last major crusader city, fell. Crusading continued in other forms for centuries, but the crusader states in the Holy Land were gone."],["Exchange and trade","The Crusades increased contact between Europe and the Islamic world. Italian cities such as Venice and Genoa grew rich carrying pilgrims, soldiers and goods. Europeans developed tastes for spices, sugar, silk and other eastern goods and learned from Arab science, medicine and mathematics."],["The Reconquista","In Spain and Portugal, Christian kingdoms spent centuries pushing back the Muslim states of al-Andalus, a struggle later called the Reconquista. It ended in 1492 when Granada fell, the same year Columbus sailed. Spanish soldiers and missionaries carried its crusading spirit overseas; they even called the Muslims they met in Southeast Asia Moros, after the Moors of Spain, a name some still use today."]],
sp:["Saladin and Richard","Saladin and Richard the Lionheart were fierce enemies who never met in person, yet stories tell of their respect for each other. When Richard fell ill, Saladin is said to have sent him fresh fruit and snow from the mountains to cool his drinks, and when Richard’s horse was killed, Saladin sent him new horses. Their truce of 1192 ended the Third Crusade."],
g:[["Crusade","A medieval religious war launched by the Church"],["Pilgrim","A person who travels to a holy place"],["Chivalry","The code of honor of medieval knights"],["Truce","An agreement to stop fighting for a time"]],
f:["The word crusade comes from the Latin word for cross.","Saladin recaptured Jerusalem in 1187.","The Fourth Crusade attacked Christian Constantinople instead of the Holy Land."],
fun:"The word crusade comes from crux, the Latin word for cross.",
tl:"Ang mga Krusada ay serye ng digmaang panrelihiyon mula 1095 sa pagitan ng mga Kristiyano ng Kanlurang Europa at mga pinunong Muslim upang masakop ang Jerusalem. Nagdulot ito ng karahasan ngunit nagpalawak din ng kalakalan at palitan ng kaalaman."});

;
/* data/gaps5.js */
// Gap articles, part 5: History (the Holocaust, the Korean War, the Vietnam War, the fall of the Soviet Union).
ADDART({id:"holocaust",t:"The Holocaust",c:"History",i:"🕯️",w:"The Holocaust",r:["ww2","judaism","hrights"],
p:[["A genocide","The Holocaust was the murder of about six million European Jews by Nazi Germany and its collaborators during World War II."],["Other victims","The Nazis also persecuted and killed Roma, disabled people, Poles, Soviet prisoners of war and others."],["Never again","The Holocaust led to new international laws against genocide and a commitment to remember its victims."]],
l:"The Holocaust was the systematic, state-sponsored persecution and murder of about six million Jews by Nazi Germany and its allies and collaborators between 1933 and 1945. Millions of others, including Roma, people with disabilities, Poles, Soviet prisoners of war, gay men and political opponents, were also persecuted or killed. It is one of the darkest events in human history, and remembering it helps guard against hatred and genocide today.",
s:[["Hatred becomes law","After Adolf Hitler and the Nazi Party came to power in Germany in 1933, they blamed Jews for the country’s problems and began excluding them from society. The Nuremberg Laws of 1935 stripped German Jews of citizenship and banned marriage between Jews and other Germans. Jews were forced out of jobs, schools and businesses."],["Kristallnacht","On the night of 9 to 10 November 1938, Nazi mobs attacked Jewish communities across Germany and Austria, burning synagogues, smashing shop windows and killing dozens of people. About 30,000 Jewish men were sent to concentration camps. The pogrom became known as Kristallnacht, the Night of Broken Glass."],["Ghettos","After Germany invaded Poland in 1939, Jews in occupied lands were forced into crowded, walled-off sections of cities called ghettos, such as those in Warsaw and Łódź. Hunger and disease killed many. In 1943, Jewish fighters in the Warsaw Ghetto rose up against the Germans and held out for weeks before the uprising was crushed."],["Mass murder","After Germany invaded the Soviet Union in 1941, mobile killing units shot more than a million Jews. In January 1942, Nazi officials met at Wannsee near Berlin to coordinate what they called the Final Solution. Jews from across Europe were deported by train to death camps, including Auschwitz-Birkenau, Treblinka, Sobibor and Belzec, where most were murdered in gas chambers."]],
k:[["Jewish victims","About 6 million"],["Period","1933–1945"],["Largest killing center","Auschwitz-Birkenau"],["Remembrance day","27 January (International Holocaust Remembrance Day)"],["Genocide Convention","1948"]],
d:[["1933","The Nazis come to power in Germany"],["1935","The Nuremberg Laws are passed"],["1938","Kristallnacht"],["1942","The Wannsee Conference coordinates the Final Solution"],["1945","Soviet troops liberate Auschwitz on 27 January"]],
s2:[["Auschwitz","Auschwitz-Birkenau, in German-occupied Poland, was the largest Nazi camp, combining forced labor and mass murder. About 1.1 million people were killed there, most of them Jews. Soviet troops liberated it on 27 January 1945, the date now observed as International Holocaust Remembrance Day. Its site is a museum and memorial."],["Other victims","The Nazis also targeted Roma and Sinti people, killing hundreds of thousands. About 250,000 people with mental or physical disabilities were murdered in so-called euthanasia programs. Millions of Soviet prisoners of war and Polish civilians died, and gay men, Jehovah’s Witnesses and political opponents were imprisoned in camps."],["Anne Frank","Anne Frank was a Jewish girl who hid with her family in a secret annex behind her father’s office in Amsterdam from 1942 to 1944. She kept a diary describing her hopes and fears. The family was betrayed and deported, and Anne died in the Bergen-Belsen camp in early 1945, aged 15. Her diary, published by her father, has been read by millions."],["Rescuers","Some people risked their lives to save Jews. The German businessman Oskar Schindler protected more than 1,000 Jewish workers. The Swedish diplomat Raoul Wallenberg issued protective papers to tens of thousands in Budapest. Israel honors such rescuers as Righteous Among the Nations."],["Justice and remembrance","After the war, Nazi leaders were tried for war crimes and crimes against humanity at Nuremberg in 1945 and 1946. In 1948 the United Nations adopted the Genocide Convention, making genocide a crime under international law. Museums, memorials and education programs around the world teach about the Holocaust so it is never forgotten."],["Doors that opened","Most countries refused to take in Jewish refugees in the 1930s, and a conference at Évian in France in 1938 ended with almost no offers of help. A few places did open their doors. Britain’s Kindertransport rescued about 10,000 Jewish children, the Dominican Republic offered land at Sosúa, the Philippines welcomed about 1,300 refugees, and about 20,000 found safety in Shanghai, one of the few places that required no visa."]],
sp:["Shanghai, a refuge","In the late 1930s, Shanghai was an international port where refugees needed no visa to land. About 20,000 Jews from Germany, Austria and Poland escaped there, some with visas issued by the Chinese consul in Vienna, Ho Feng-Shan. During the Japanese occupation they were forced into a crowded district of Hongkou, but most survived the war. Today a museum in the old synagogue tells their story."],
g:[["Genocide","The deliberate killing of a large group of people because of their identity"],["Ghetto","A walled-off area where Jews were forced to live"],["Pogrom","A violent attack on a minority community"],["Antisemitism","Hatred of or prejudice against Jews"]],
f:["About six million Jews were murdered in the Holocaust.","International Holocaust Remembrance Day is 27 January.","About 20,000 Jewish refugees found safety in Shanghai during World War II."],
fun:"The Philippines welcomed about 1,300 Jewish refugees fleeing Nazi Europe between 1937 and 1941.",
tl:"Ang Holocaust ay ang sistematikong pagpatay ng Nazi Germany sa mga anim na milyong Hudyo at milyon-milyong iba pa noong Ikalawang Digmaang Pandaigdig. Tumanggap ang Pilipinas ng mga 1,300 Hudyong refugee bago ang digmaan."});

ADDART({id:"koreanwar",t:"The Korean War",c:"History",i:"🎖️",w:"Korean War",r:["korea","coldwar","un"],
p:[["A divided peninsula","The Korean War began in 1950 when communist North Korea invaded South Korea."],["A UN war","Troops from 16 countries fought under the United Nations flag."],["No peace treaty","The fighting stopped with an armistice in 1953, but no peace treaty has ever been signed."]],
l:"The Korean War was fought from 1950 to 1953 between communist North Korea, supported by China and the Soviet Union, and South Korea, supported by the United Nations led by the United States. It was one of the first major conflicts of the Cold War. Millions died, the peninsula was devastated and the war ended where it began, near the 38th parallel, with Korea still divided today.",
s:[["The invasion","On 25 June 1950, North Korean forces crossed the 38th parallel and swept south, capturing Seoul within days. The United Nations Security Council, with the Soviet Union absent, called on members to help South Korea. Sixteen countries sent combat troops and others sent medical units, under the command of US General Douglas MacArthur."],["Inchon and the advance north","By September, UN and South Korean forces were trapped in a small corner around Pusan. On 15 September 1950, MacArthur launched a daring landing at Inchon, near Seoul, cutting off the North Korean army. UN forces recaptured Seoul and pushed north almost to the Chinese border."],["China enters","In October 1950, hundreds of thousands of Chinese troops crossed into Korea and drove UN forces back south in bitter winter fighting. Seoul changed hands again. By mid-1951, the front settled near the 38th parallel, and the war became a deadly stalemate of trenches and hill battles."],["The armistice","Peace talks dragged on for two years, partly over the return of prisoners. On 27 July 1953, an armistice was signed at Panmunjom, ending the fighting. A Demilitarized Zone about 4 kilometers wide and 250 kilometers long was created between the two Koreas. Because no peace treaty was ever signed, the two countries remain technically at war."]],
k:[["Dates","25 June 1950 – 27 July 1953"],["Sides","North Korea and China vs South Korea and the UN"],["UN combat nations","16"],["Countries sending combat troops","16 under the UN flag"],["Deaths","About 2.5–3 million, many civilians"]],
d:[["1950","North Korea invades South Korea on 25 June"],["1950","UN forces land at Inchon on 15 September"],["1950","China enters the war in October"],["1951","Seoul changes hands for the fourth time"],["1953","An armistice is signed on 27 July"]],
s2:[["Sixteen nations under the UN flag","The United States sent by far the most troops, but 15 other countries also sent combat forces: the United Kingdom, Canada, Türkiye, Australia, France, Greece, the Netherlands, New Zealand, the Philippines, Thailand, Ethiopia, Colombia, Belgium, Luxembourg and South Africa. Others, such as India, Denmark, Norway and Sweden, sent medical teams."],["The Battle of Yultong","In November 1950, as UN troops pushed toward the Chinese border, about 120,000 Chinese soldiers surrounded American and allied forces at the Chosin Reservoir in the frozen mountains of North Korea. In temperatures below −30 °C, the trapped troops fought their way out over 17 days to the port of Hungnam, where ships evacuated about 100,000 soldiers and 100,000 civilians."],["Future leaders","The war and the border that followed split millions of Korean families. Many fled south and never saw their parents, brothers or sisters again. Since 1985, the two Koreas have held occasional reunions, where elderly relatives meet for a few hours after decades apart. Tens of thousands have died still waiting for a chance to see their families."],["The human cost","The war killed millions of people, most of them Korean civilians. Cities and villages were bombed to ruins, and millions became refugees. Families were separated by the new border, and many never saw their relatives again. Several hundred thousand Chinese soldiers and about 36,000 Americans also died."],["The Cold War heats up","The Korean War showed that the Cold War between the United States and the Soviet Union could turn into real fighting. The United States greatly increased its military spending and alliances in Asia, including its defense ties with Japan and South Korea. The war also strengthened the communist alliance of China and North Korea."],["The DMZ today","The Demilitarized Zone between the Koreas is one of the most heavily guarded borders in the world, lined with fences, mines and soldiers. At the Joint Security Area in Panmunjom, soldiers from both sides stand face to face. Ironically, the lack of human activity has made the DMZ a refuge for wildlife, including rare cranes."]],
sp:["Reporting from the front","Many journalists risked their lives to cover the war. The American reporter Marguerite Higgins landed with the troops at Inchon and reported from the front lines despite orders banning women correspondents. In 1951 she became the first woman to win the Pulitzer Prize for international reporting. Several reporters were killed, and photographs from the war shaped how the world saw it."],
g:[["Armistice","An agreement to stop fighting"],["Demilitarized zone","A strip of land where military forces are not allowed"],["Parallel","An imaginary line around Earth at a given latitude"],["Stalemate","A situation in which neither side can win"]],
f:["Sixteen countries sent combat troops to fight under the UN flag in Korea.","The Korean War ended with an armistice, not a peace treaty.","The DMZ has become a refuge for rare wildlife."],
fun:"The two Koreas are technically still at war, because the 1953 armistice was never followed by a peace treaty.",
tl:"Ang Digmaang Koreano mula 1950 hanggang 1953 ay sa pagitan ng Hilagang Korea at Tsina laban sa Timog Korea at United Nations. Nagpadala ang Pilipinas ng mga 7,400 sundalo, at natapos ito sa armistice, hindi sa kasunduang pangkapayapaan."});

ADDART({id:"vietnam",t:"The Vietnam War",c:"History",i:"🚁",w:"Vietnam War",r:["coldwar","sea","asean"],
p:[["A long war","The Vietnam War was fought from about 1955 to 1975 between communist North Vietnam and US-backed South Vietnam."],["A Cold War conflict","The United States sent more than half a million troops at the war’s height."],["Reunification","The war ended in 1975 when North Vietnamese forces captured Saigon and reunited the country."]],
l:"The Vietnam War was a long and devastating conflict in Vietnam, Laos and Cambodia, fought mainly between communist North Vietnam and its southern allies, the Viet Cong, and the government of South Vietnam, supported by the United States. Part of the Cold War, it lasted about two decades, killed millions of people and divided public opinion around the world before ending with the fall of Saigon in 1975.",
s:[["Background","Vietnam was a French colony for decades. After World War II, Vietnamese nationalists and communists led by Ho Chi Minh fought for independence and defeated the French at Dien Bien Phu in 1954. The Geneva Accords divided Vietnam temporarily at the 17th parallel, with a communist North and an anti-communist South. Promised elections to reunite the country were never held."],["American involvement","The United States feared that if South Vietnam fell to communism, other countries in Southeast Asia would follow, an idea called the domino theory. It sent advisers and aid, and after a reported attack on US ships in the Gulf of Tonkin in 1964, began sending large numbers of troops. By 1969 more than 540,000 Americans were in Vietnam."],["A different kind of war","The war had no clear front lines. Viet Cong guerrillas fought in jungles, rice paddies and villages and moved supplies along the Ho Chi Minh Trail through Laos and Cambodia. The United States used massive bombing, helicopters and chemicals such as Agent Orange to destroy forests, which harmed civilians and soldiers for generations."],["The Tet Offensive","In January 1968, during the Vietnamese New Year, Tet, North Vietnamese and Viet Cong forces attacked cities across South Vietnam, even reaching the US embassy in Saigon. Although they suffered heavy losses, the offensive shocked Americans, who had been told the war was being won, and turned many against it."]],
k:[["Period","About 1955–1975"],["Sides","North Vietnam and Viet Cong vs South Vietnam and the US"],["US troops at peak","More than 540,000 (1969)"],["Deaths","Over 1 million Vietnamese; about 58,000 Americans"],["Ended","Fall of Saigon, 30 April 1975"]],
d:[["1954","The French are defeated at Dien Bien Phu"],["1964","The Gulf of Tonkin incident leads to a major US buildup"],["1968","The Tet Offensive"],["1973","The Paris Peace Accords; US troops withdraw"],["1975","Saigon falls on 30 April"]],
s2:[["Protest and peace talks","As casualties mounted and television brought the war into homes, protests grew in the United States and around the world. Many young Americans resisted being drafted. Peace talks in Paris led to the Paris Peace Accords in January 1973, and the last US combat troops left Vietnam that year."],["The fall of Saigon","Fighting continued between North and South Vietnam. In early 1975, North Vietnamese forces swept south. On 30 April 1975, their tanks crashed through the gates of the presidential palace in Saigon, and South Vietnam surrendered. Helicopters carried the last Americans and some Vietnamese from the US embassy roof. Saigon was renamed Ho Chi Minh City."],["The human cost","Estimates of Vietnamese deaths, soldiers and civilians, range from over one million to more than three million. About 58,000 Americans died. Millions were wounded or displaced, and unexploded bombs and the effects of Agent Orange continued to harm people for decades. The war also spread into Laos and Cambodia."],["Allies on both sides","South Vietnam and the United States were joined by troops from South Korea, which sent more than 300,000 soldiers over the course of the war, Australia, New Zealand and Thailand, while the Philippines sent engineers and medical teams. North Vietnam received weapons, supplies and advisers from the Soviet Union and China."],["The boat people","After 1975, hundreds of thousands of Vietnamese fled by sea in small, crowded boats, facing storms and pirates. Many reached Malaysia, Thailand, Hong Kong, Indonesia and the Philippines, where camps sheltered them until they were resettled in the United States, Canada, Australia, France and other countries."],["Vietnam today","After the war, Vietnam faced poverty and isolation. Economic reforms called Doi Moi, begun in 1986, opened the economy, and Vietnam became one of the fastest-growing countries in Asia. It joined ASEAN in 1995, the same year it restored diplomatic relations with the United States, and is now an important trading partner of many countries."]],
sp:["Camps for the boat people","From the late 1970s, refugee camps across Southeast Asia sheltered people fleeing Vietnam, Cambodia and Laos. Pulau Bidong in Malaysia, Galang in Indonesia and a processing center in Bataan in the Philippines each housed tens of thousands at a time, where refugees learned new languages and prepared to resettle abroad. In 1979 an international conference in Geneva agreed to share the task of resettling them."],
g:[["Domino theory","The belief that if one country fell to communism, its neighbors would follow"],["Guerrilla","A fighter in small, irregular groups using surprise attacks"],["Draft","A system for calling people into military service"],["Refugee","A person forced to flee their country"]],
f:["Saigon was renamed Ho Chi Minh City after 1975.","About 800,000 Vietnamese boat people reached other countries between 1975 and 1995.","Vietnam joined ASEAN in 1995."],
fun:"The Philippine Refugee Processing Center in Bataan sheltered hundreds of thousands of refugees from Vietnam, Cambodia and Laos.",
tl:"Ang Digmaang Vietnam ay mahabang labanan mula mga 1955 hanggang 1975 sa pagitan ng komunistang Hilagang Vietnam at Timog Vietnam na sinuportahan ng Estados Unidos. Natapos ito nang bumagsak ang Saigon noong 1975, at maraming refugee ang kinupkop ng Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"ussrfall",t:"The Fall of the Soviet Union",c:"History",i:"🧱",w:"Dissolution of the Soviet Union",r:["coldwar","ww2","un"],
p:[["A superpower collapses","In 1991 the Soviet Union, the world’s largest country, broke apart into 15 independent states."],["The Cold War ends","The collapse ended more than four decades of rivalry with the United States."],["Walls come down","The fall of the Berlin Wall in 1989 symbolized the end of communist rule in Eastern Europe."]],
l:"The Soviet Union, or USSR, was a communist superpower that stretched across Europe and Asia from 1922 to 1991. In the late 1980s, economic troubles, demands for freedom and reforms by its leader Mikhail Gorbachev led to the collapse of communist governments across Eastern Europe and then of the Soviet Union itself. Its breakup into 15 countries in December 1991 ended the Cold War and reshaped the world.",
s:[["The Soviet Union","The Union of Soviet Socialist Republics was formed in 1922 after the Russian Revolution. It was ruled by the Communist Party, which controlled the economy, media and daily life. Under Joseph Stalin, millions died in famines, purges and labor camps. After World War II, the Soviet Union controlled Eastern Europe and became a nuclear superpower rival to the United States."],["Gorbachev’s reforms","By the 1980s the Soviet economy was struggling, and the war in Afghanistan was costly. Mikhail Gorbachev, who became leader in 1985, introduced glasnost, openness, allowing more free speech, and perestroika, restructuring, to reform the economy. The 1986 Chernobyl nuclear disaster exposed the system’s secrecy and failures."],["1989: Eastern Europe breaks free","In 1989, peaceful protests and elections swept away communist governments in Poland, Hungary, East Germany, Czechoslovakia, Bulgaria and Romania. On 9 November 1989, the Berlin Wall, which had divided the city since 1961, was opened, and joyful crowds began tearing it down. Germany was reunited in October 1990."],["The final collapse","Soviet republics such as Lithuania, Latvia and Estonia demanded independence. In August 1991, hard-line communists tried to overthrow Gorbachev, but crowds in Moscow led by Boris Yeltsin resisted, and the coup failed. In December the leaders of Russia, Ukraine and Belarus declared the Soviet Union dissolved. Gorbachev resigned on 25 December 1991, and the Soviet flag was lowered over the Kremlin."]],
k:[["Existed","1922–1991"],["Successor states","15"],["Last leader","Mikhail Gorbachev"],["Berlin Wall opened","9 November 1989"],["Dissolved","26 December 1991"]],
d:[["1985","Mikhail Gorbachev becomes Soviet leader"],["1986","The Chernobyl nuclear disaster"],["1989","The Berlin Wall falls on 9 November"],["1991","A hard-line coup attempt fails in August"],["1991","Gorbachev resigns on 25 December; the USSR is dissolved"]],
s2:[["Fifteen new countries","The Soviet Union split into 15 independent countries: Russia, Ukraine, Belarus, Moldova, Lithuania, Latvia, Estonia, Georgia, Armenia, Azerbaijan, Kazakhstan, Uzbekistan, Turkmenistan, Kyrgyzstan and Tajikistan. Russia, the largest, took over the Soviet seat on the UN Security Council and its nuclear weapons."],["Hard times","The 1990s were difficult for many former Soviet citizens. The switch to a market economy brought high inflation, unemployment and the collapse of savings, while a few people became extremely rich. Life expectancy fell in Russia. Some countries, such as the Baltic states, joined the European Union and NATO in 2004."],["The end of the Cold War","With the Soviet Union gone, the Cold War between communist and capitalist blocs ended. The threat of nuclear war between superpowers eased, and arms reduction treaties cut nuclear stockpiles. Gorbachev received the Nobel Peace Prize in 1990 for his role in ending the Cold War peacefully."],["Russia after 1991","Boris Yeltsin became Russia’s first president. Vladimir Putin took power in 2000 and has led Russia since, as president or prime minister, strengthening central control. In 2014 Russia annexed Crimea from Ukraine, and in 2022 it launched a full-scale invasion of Ukraine, the largest war in Europe since 1945."],["Remembering the Wall","Pieces of the Berlin Wall stand in museums and parks around the world as symbols of freedom. A short stretch in Berlin, the East Side Gallery, is covered in murals by artists. Every 9 November, Germans remember the night the Wall opened, which also marks darker anniversaries in German history."],["Giving up nuclear weapons","When the Soviet Union broke apart, thousands of nuclear weapons were left in Ukraine, Belarus and Kazakhstan. All three agreed to give them up and send them to Russia. In the Budapest Memorandum of 1994, Russia, the United States and the United Kingdom promised to respect Ukraine’s borders in return, a promise that became bitterly disputed after Russia’s actions in 2014 and 2022."]],
sp:["The night the Wall opened","On the evening of 9 November 1989, an East German official mistakenly announced at a press conference that citizens could cross the border immediately. Thousands rushed to the checkpoints in Berlin, and overwhelmed guards opened the gates. People climbed on the Wall, hugged strangers and chipped off pieces as souvenirs. Within a year, Germany was reunited."],
g:[["Communism","A system in which the state owns property and controls the economy"],["Glasnost","Openness, Gorbachev’s policy of freer speech"],["Perestroika","Restructuring, Gorbachev’s economic reforms"],["Coup","A sudden, illegal seizure of power"]],
f:["The Soviet Union broke into 15 countries.","The Berlin Wall stood from 1961 to 1989.","Gorbachev won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1990."],
fun:"The Berlin Wall opened in 1989 partly because an official misread his notes at a press conference.",
tl:"Ang Unyong Sobyet ay komunistang bansa mula 1922 hanggang 1991. Matapos ang mga reporma ni Gorbachev at pagbagsak ng Berlin Wall noong 1989, nahati ito sa 15 malayang bansa noong Disyembre 1991, na nagwakas sa Cold War."});

;
/* data/gaps6.js */
// Gap articles, part 6: How We Live (democracy, human rights, law and courts, chess, volleyball).
ADDART({id:"democ",t:"Democracy and Government",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🗳️",w:"Democracy",r:["phgov","hrights","law"],
p:[["Rule by the people","Democracy means government by the people, who choose their leaders in free elections."],["Many kinds of government","Countries may be democracies, monarchies, dictatorships or mixtures of these."],["Rights and duties","Democracy depends on free speech, fair elections, the rule of law and citizens who take part."]],
l:"Government is the system by which a country or community is ruled. In a democracy, power comes from the people, who choose their leaders in regular, free and fair elections and can replace them peacefully. Democracy began in ancient Athens and has spread around the world. It depends not only on voting but also on protected rights, independent courts, a free press and active citizens.",
s:[["The word","Democracy comes from the Greek words demos, people, and kratos, rule. In ancient Athens, from about 508 BCE, male citizens met in an assembly to vote directly on laws and decisions. Women, enslaved people and foreigners could not take part, but Athens gave the world the idea of rule by citizens."],["Kinds of government","In a democracy, the people choose their leaders. In a monarchy, a king or queen rules, though in constitutional monarchies such as Japan and the United Kingdom, elected parliaments hold real power. In a dictatorship or authoritarian state, one person or group holds power without free elections. Some countries mix features of different systems."],["Direct and representative","In a direct democracy, citizens vote on laws themselves, as in ancient Athens or in Swiss referendums today. Most modern democracies are representative: citizens elect lawmakers and leaders to make decisions for them. Some, like the United States, Brazil and Indonesia, have presidents elected separately from the legislature; others, like the United Kingdom, India and Japan, choose a prime minister from parliament."],["What democracy needs","Elections alone do not make a democracy. Democracies also need the rule of law, so that everyone, including leaders, obeys the same laws; protection of human rights such as free speech and assembly; separation of powers among branches of government; independent courts; a free press; and peaceful transfers of power after elections."]],
k:[["Word origin","Greek demos (people) + kratos (rule)"],["Born in","Athens, about 508 BCE"],["First women’s national vote","New Zealand, 1893"],["Largest democracy","India, nearly 1 billion voters"],["International Day of Democracy","15 September"]],
d:[["c. 508 BCE","Cleisthenes introduces democratic reforms in Athens"],["1215","King John of England agrees to the Magna Carta"],["1787","The United States Constitution is written"],["1893","New Zealand becomes the first country to give women the vote in national elections"],["1971","Swiss women win the right to vote in national elections"]],
s2:[["Limiting kings","Over centuries, people found ways to limit the power of rulers. In 1215, English barons forced King John to sign the Magna Carta, which said even the king must obey the law. Parliaments grew in power, and the American and French revolutions of the late 1700s declared that governments get their authority from the people."],["Votes for all","At first, only men who owned property could vote in most democracies. Over time, the vote was extended to all men, to women and to people of all races. New Zealand gave women the vote in 1893, Finland in 1906, the United States in 1920 and France only in 1944, while Switzerland gave women the national vote in 1971."],["Democracy around the world","Democracy has spread in waves. After World War II, democracies were rebuilt in Western Europe and Japan, and India became the world’s largest democracy. From the 1970s, Spain, Portugal, much of Latin America, the Philippines, South Korea, South Africa and Eastern Europe moved from dictatorship to democracy. Today about half the world’s countries are rated as democracies, though many face challenges."],["Political parties and elections","In most democracies, political parties compete for votes by offering different ideas and candidates. Elections must be free, with secret ballots, and fair, with honest counting and equal rules. Independent election commissions, such as India’s Election Commission, and observers help protect the vote."],["Being a good citizen","Democracy works best when citizens take part. This means voting, staying informed from reliable sources, respecting others’ rights and views, obeying laws, paying taxes and joining community activities. Young people can take part through student councils, youth parliaments and youth groups."],["Challenges","Democracies face challenges such as corruption, misinformation, low trust in institutions, political violence and leaders who try to weaken courts or the press. Studies show that democracy has declined in some countries in recent years. Many people work to strengthen it through education, civic participation and protecting free and fair elections."]],
sp:["Government of the people","In 1863, at Gettysburg during the American Civil War, President Abraham Lincoln described democracy as government of the people, by the people, for the people. The phrase is among the most famous definitions of democracy. Winston Churchill later joked that democracy is the worst form of government except all the others that have been tried."],
g:[["Democracy","Government by the people through free elections"],["Monarchy","Rule by a king or queen"],["Suffrage","The right to vote"],["Rule of law","The principle that everyone must obey the law"]],
f:["Democracy comes from Greek words meaning rule by the people.","New Zealand was the first country to give women the national vote, in 1893.","Switzerland gave women the national vote only in 1971."],
fun:"In 1937, more than 447,000 Filipino women voted in a plebiscite to win the right to vote.",
tl:"Ang demokrasya ay pamahalaan ng mamamayan, na pumipili ng kanilang mga pinuno sa malaya at patas na halalan. Kailangan din nito ang paggalang sa karapatang pantao, malayang pamamahayag at aktibong pakikilahok ng mamamayan."});

ADDART({id:"hrights",t:"Human Rights",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🤝",w:"Human rights",r:["un","democ","law"],
p:[["Rights for everyone","Human rights are the basic rights and freedoms that belong to every person in the world."],["A universal declaration","The Universal Declaration of Human Rights was adopted by the United Nations in 1948."],["Protecting dignity","Human rights protect people from abuse and guarantee freedoms such as speech, religion and education."]],
l:"Human rights are rights that every person has simply because they are human, whatever their nationality, sex, ethnicity, religion, language or other status. They include the right to life, liberty and security, freedom from torture and slavery, freedom of thought, speech and religion, the right to a fair trial, and rights to education, health and work. They are protected by international agreements and national constitutions and laws.",
s:[["The Universal Declaration","After the horrors of World War II, the United Nations adopted the Universal Declaration of Human Rights on 10 December 1948. A committee chaired by Eleanor Roosevelt drafted it. Its 30 articles begin: All human beings are born free and equal in dignity and rights. It has been translated into more than 500 languages, more than any other document."],["Kinds of rights","Civil and political rights include the right to life, freedom of speech and religion, freedom from torture, the right to vote and the right to a fair trial. Economic, social and cultural rights include the rights to work, education, health care, food and housing. All rights are seen as connected and equally important."],["Treaties","The Universal Declaration is not itself a binding treaty, but it led to many that are. The two most important, adopted in 1966, cover civil and political rights and economic, social and cultural rights. Others protect children, women, people with disabilities and migrant workers and ban torture and racial discrimination."],["Rights of the child","The Convention on the Rights of the Child, adopted in 1989, is the most widely ratified human rights treaty in history. It says children have the right to life, a name and nationality, education, health care, play, protection from abuse and exploitation, and to have their views heard. Every UN member except the United States has ratified it."]],
k:[["Universal Declaration adopted","10 December 1948"],["Articles","30"],["Human Rights Day","10 December"],["Rights of the Child convention","1989"],["Regional courts","Europe, the Americas and Africa"]],
d:[["1948","The Universal Declaration of Human Rights is adopted"],["1961","Amnesty International is founded"],["1966","Two major human rights covenants are adopted"],["1950","The European Convention on Human Rights is signed"],["1989","The Convention on the Rights of the Child is adopted"]],
s2:[["Eleanor Roosevelt","Eleanor Roosevelt, former First Lady of the United States, chaired the UN commission that drafted the Universal Declaration. Working with experts from many countries, including René Cassin of France, Charles Malik of Lebanon, P. C. Chang of China and Hansa Mehta of India, she guided the debates to a final text. She called the declaration a Magna Carta for all humanity."],["Human rights institutions","Many countries have national human rights commissions to investigate abuses, and regional courts protect rights across borders, such as the European Court of Human Rights, the Inter-American Court of Human Rights and the African Court on Human and Peoples’ Rights. The UN Human Rights Council reviews the record of every country in turn."],["Equality and discrimination","Human rights forbid discrimination on grounds such as race, sex, religion, disability or social status. The Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination against Women, adopted in 1979, protects women’s rights, and the Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities, adopted in 2006, protects people with disabilities."],["Defenders","Human rights defenders are people who work peacefully to protect rights, including lawyers, journalists, community leaders and organizations such as Amnesty International, founded in 1961, and Human Rights Watch. Journalists Maria Ressa of the Philippines and Dmitry Muratov of Russia shared the Nobel Peace Prize in 2021 for defending freedom of expression."],["Rights and responsibilities","Having rights also means respecting the rights of others. Freedom of speech, for example, does not allow inciting violence or spreading lies that harm people. Governments have the main duty to protect human rights, but individuals, companies and communities also have responsibilities."],["Human Rights Day","Every 10 December, the anniversary of the Universal Declaration, the world marks Human Rights Day. Schools and organizations around the world hold activities, and governments and civil society reflect on progress and challenges. Learning about human rights helps people know and defend their rights and those of others."]],
sp:["Article 1","The first article of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights reads: All human beings are born free and equal in dignity and rights. They are endowed with reason and conscience and should act towards one another in a spirit of brotherhood. These words express the idea that every person deserves respect and fair treatment."],
g:[["Human rights","Rights and freedoms belonging to every person"],["Covenant","A formal binding agreement between countries"],["Ratify","To formally approve a treaty and agree to follow it"],["Discrimination","Unfair treatment because of who someone is"]],
f:["The Universal Declaration of Human Rights has been translated into more than 500 languages.","The Convention on the Rights of the Child is the most widely ratified human rights treaty.","Human Rights Day is 10 December."],
fun:"The Universal Declaration of Human Rights is the world’s most translated document.",
tl:"Ang karapatang pantao ay mga batayang karapatan at kalayaan ng bawat tao, gaya ng karapatan sa buhay, malayang pananalita at edukasyon. Pinagtibay ng United Nations ang Pandaigdigang Pahayag ng mga Karapatang Pantao noong 1948."});

ADDART({id:"law",t:"Law and Courts",c:"Economics and Society",i:"⚖️",w:"Law",r:["democ","hrights","phgov"],
p:[["Rules for society","Laws are rules made by a government that everyone in a country must follow."],["Courts and justice","Courts decide disputes and whether people accused of crimes are guilty."],["The rule of law","No one, not even leaders, is above the law."]],
l:"Law is the system of rules that a society creates and enforces to keep order, protect rights and settle disputes. Laws are made by legislatures, explained and applied by courts and enforced by police and other officials. From the ancient Code of Hammurabi to modern constitutions, law has shaped how people live together, and legal systems around the world draw on many traditions.",
s:[["Why we have laws","Laws protect people’s safety, property and rights; set rules for business, families and government; and provide peaceful ways to resolve conflicts. Without laws, disputes might be settled by force. Good laws are clear, apply equally to everyone and are made through fair processes."],["Kinds of law","Criminal law deals with offenses against society, such as theft and murder, which the state prosecutes and punishes. Civil law deals with disputes between people or organizations, such as contracts, property and family matters. Constitutional law covers how government works and the rights of citizens, and international law governs relations between countries."],["Two great traditions","Many countries follow civil law, based on written codes descended from Roman law, as in Spain, France, Germany and most of Latin America. Others follow common law, developed in England, in which judges’ past decisions, called precedents, guide future cases, as in the United States, Canada, Australia and India. Some countries, such as South Africa and the Philippines, have mixed systems."],["Courts","Courts hear cases and decide them according to the law. In a trial, each side presents evidence and arguments, and a judge, or in some countries a jury of ordinary citizens, decides. Higher courts hear appeals from lower ones, and a supreme or constitutional court has the final word in most countries."]],
k:[["Oldest famous law code","Code of Hammurabi, c. 1754 BCE"],["Main legal traditions","Civil law, common law, religious and customary law"],["World court","International Court of Justice, The Hague"],["Revised Penal Code","1930"],["International Criminal Court","Founded 2002"]],
d:[["c. 1754 BCE","King Hammurabi of Babylon issues his law code"],["c. 450 BCE","Rome’s Twelve Tables are written"],["534 CE","The Code of Justinian is completed"],["1804","The Napoleonic Code becomes law in France"],["2002","The International Criminal Court is established"]],
s2:[["Hammurabi’s code","Around 1754 BCE, King Hammurabi of Babylon had 282 laws carved on a tall stone pillar for all to see. They covered trade, property, family and crime, and included the famous principle of an eye for an eye. The pillar is now in the Louvre Museum in Paris. It is one of the oldest written law codes known."],["Rights of the accused","Modern legal systems protect people accused of crimes. In many countries, the accused is presumed innocent until proven guilty beyond reasonable doubt, has the right to a lawyer and to remain silent, and cannot be tortured or forced to confess. These rights protect against wrongful punishment."],["Sharia courts","In some countries, religious law governs parts of life. Islamic law, or sharia, shapes family law in many Muslim-majority countries, and countries such as India, Israel, Kenya and the Philippines let religious communities follow their own rules on marriage, divorce and inheritance, sometimes through special courts. Jewish courts and Catholic church tribunals also decide some religious matters for their members."],["Barangay justice","Many disputes are settled without a trial. In mediation, a neutral person helps both sides reach an agreement. Many countries have community or village systems for this, such as panchayats in India, village councils in parts of Africa and barangay conciliation panels in the Philippines. Settling out of court saves time and money and keeps communities peaceful."],["Children and the law","Most countries set a minimum age of criminal responsibility, from about 10 in England to 14 in Germany and Japan and higher in some countries. International standards say children in conflict with the law should be helped and rehabilitated rather than simply punished, and other laws protect children from abuse, exploitation and child labor."],["International law","International law is made by treaties and customs among countries. The International Court of Justice in The Hague settles disputes between states, and the International Criminal Court, set up in 2002, tries people accused of genocide and war crimes. Tribunals under the UN Convention on the Law of the Sea settle disputes about the oceans."]],
sp:["Becoming a lawyer","How people become lawyers differs from country to country. In the United States, students take a university degree, then three years of law school, and then a state bar examination. In Britain and much of Europe, law can be studied as a first degree, followed by professional training. Japan and South Korea have notoriously hard bar exams. Lawyers can become judges, prosecutors, public defenders or advocates."],
g:[["Legislature","The body that makes laws, such as Congress"],["Precedent","An earlier court decision used as a guide"],["Appeal","A request for a higher court to review a decision"],["Presumption of innocence","The rule that an accused person is innocent until proven guilty"]],
f:["Hammurabi’s 282 laws were carved on a stone pillar.","The Napoleonic Code of 1804 shaped the laws of many countries.","The International Criminal Court was set up in 2002."],
fun:"Hammurabi’s 282 laws, carved in stone around 1754 BCE, can still be seen in the Louvre in Paris.",
tl:"Ang batas ay mga patakarang ginagawa ng pamahalaan na dapat sundin ng lahat, at ang mga hukuman ang nagpapasya sa mga kaso. Pinaghalong tradisyong Espanyol, Amerikano at Islamiko ang sistemang legal ng Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"chess",t:"Chess",c:"Sports",i:"♟️",w:"Chess",r:["olymp","prob","ai"],
p:[["A game of strategy","Chess is a board game for two players, each commanding an army of 16 pieces."],["Ancient origins","It began in India around 1,500 years ago and spread through Persia and the Arab world to Europe."],["A game for everyone","Chess is played in almost every country, in schools, clubs, parks and online. Grandmasters have come from every continent, and talented young players can now reach a high level at an early age."]],
l:"Chess is a strategy game played on a board of 64 squares between two players, each with 16 pieces: a king, a queen, two rooks, two bishops, two knights and eight pawns. The goal is to checkmate the opponent’s king. Played for about 1,500 years, chess is enjoyed by hundreds of millions of people, from children in schools to world champions.",
s:[["The pieces","The king moves one square in any direction. The queen, the most powerful piece, moves any distance in any straight line. Rooks move in straight lines along ranks and files, bishops diagonally and knights in an L-shape, jumping over other pieces. Pawns move forward and capture diagonally, and a pawn reaching the far side can be promoted, usually to a queen."],["Check and checkmate","A king that is under attack is in check and must escape. If it cannot escape, it is checkmate and the game is over. A game can also end in a draw, for example by stalemate, when a player has no legal move but is not in check, or by agreement. The word checkmate comes from the Persian shah mat, the king is helpless."],["Origins","Chess began in India around the 500s CE as chaturanga, a game representing an army with infantry, cavalry, elephants and chariots. It spread to Persia as shatranj and then through the Islamic world to Europe. Around 1475, in Spain and Italy, the queen and bishop gained their powerful modern moves, creating the game played today."],["Competition","Chess tournaments use clocks to limit thinking time. Players earn ratings based on their results, and the best can earn the title of grandmaster from FIDE, the international chess federation founded in 1924. The Chess Olympiad brings national teams together every two years."]],
k:[["Board","64 squares"],["Pieces per player","16"],["Origin","India, about the 500s CE"],["World federation","FIDE, founded 1924"],["First official world champion","Wilhelm Steinitz, 1886"]],
d:[["c. 600","Chaturanga is played in India"],["1886","The first official World Chess Championship is held"],["1974","Eugene Torre becomes Asia’s first grandmaster"],["1978","Karpov and Korchnoi play the World Championship in Baguio"],["1997","IBM’s Deep Blue defeats world champion Garry Kasparov"]],
s2:[["World champions","The first official world champion was Wilhelm Steinitz in 1886. Famous champions include José Raúl Capablanca, Bobby Fischer, Garry Kasparov and Magnus Carlsen, who held the title from 2013 to 2023. In 2024, Gukesh Dommaraju of India became the youngest undisputed world champion at 18."],["Chess around the world","Chess is played in almost every country. India, Russia, China and the United States are strong chess nations, and Viswanathan Anand of India won the world title in 2000, helping launch a boom in his country. Online chess has made the game more popular than ever, with tens of millions of people playing every day."],["The Fischer–Spassky match","In 1972 in Reykjavik, Iceland, the American Bobby Fischer beat the Soviet champion Boris Spassky by 12½ points to 8½ to win the World Chess Championship. The match, played at the height of the Cold War, was followed around the world and ended the Soviet Union’s long hold on the title. Fischer, a former child prodigy, never defended his crown."],["Computers and chess","In 1997 IBM’s computer Deep Blue defeated world champion Garry Kasparov in a match, a landmark for artificial intelligence. Today chess programs on phones play far better than any human, and players use them to study. In 2017 an AI program called AlphaZero taught itself chess in hours and beat the strongest programs."],["Benefits of chess","Chess exercises memory, concentration, planning and problem-solving, and teaches players to think ahead and learn from mistakes. Many schools teach chess for these reasons. It can be played by people of any age or physical ability, and online chess lets people play opponents around the world at any time."],["A game of endless possibilities","The number of possible chess games is astronomically large, estimated at more than 10 to the power of 120, far more than the number of atoms in the observable universe. After just three moves each, there are more than 9 million possible games. No two serious games are ever likely to be exactly the same."]],
sp:["The battle of Baguio","The 1978 World Championship in Baguio was one of the strangest in chess history. Korchnoi’s team complained that a parapsychologist in Karpov’s camp was trying to hypnotize him, and Korchnoi wore mirrored glasses in response. Players argued about chairs, flags and even the flavor of yogurt. After 32 games, Karpov kept his title."],
g:[["Checkmate","When the king is attacked and cannot escape, ending the game"],["Grandmaster","The highest title awarded to chess players"],["Stalemate","A draw when a player has no legal move but is not in check"],["Promotion","Turning a pawn into another piece when it reaches the far side"]],
f:["The word checkmate comes from Persian words meaning the king is helpless.","Eugene Torre was Asia’s first grandmaster.","The 1978 World Chess Championship was played in Baguio."],
fun:"The 1978 World Chess Championship in Baguio included a dispute over the flavor of a yogurt.",
tl:"Ang chess ay larong pang-estratehiya ng dalawang manlalaro sa tabla na may 64 na parisukat. Nagmula ito sa India, at ang layunin ay i-checkmate ang hari ng kalaban."});

ADDART({id:"volley",t:"Volleyball",c:"Sports",i:"🏐",w:"Volleyball",r:["bball","olymp","phsport"],
p:[["Over the net","Volleyball is a team sport in which two teams of six hit a ball over a high net."],["Invented in 1895","It was created by William G. Morgan in the United States as a gentler alternative to basketball."],["Around the world","Volleyball is especially popular in Brazil, Italy, Poland, Japan, Türkiye, Serbia and the Philippines."]],
l:"Volleyball is a team sport in which two teams of six players try to send a ball over a net so that it lands in the opponent’s court, using no more than three touches per side. Invented in the United States in 1895, it is played by hundreds of millions of people worldwide, indoors and on beaches.",
s:[["How it is played","A team scores a point when the ball lands in the opponent’s court or the opponents make an error. Each side may touch the ball up to three times before sending it back, usually with a pass, a set and an attack. A player may not touch the ball twice in a row. Matches are usually best of five sets, played to 25 points."],["Positions","Players rotate positions each time their team wins the serve back. Setters direct the attack, outside and opposite hitters attack, middle blockers block at the net, and the libero, wearing a different color, specializes in defense and passing. The libero position was introduced in 1998."],["Skills","The main skills are serving, passing or receiving with the forearms, setting the ball with the fingertips, attacking or spiking by hitting the ball hard downward, blocking at the net and digging, saving hard-hit balls near the floor. Good teamwork and communication are essential."],["Origins","In 1895 William G. Morgan, a physical education director at a YMCA in Holyoke, Massachusetts, invented a game he called mintonette, combining elements of basketball, tennis and handball, for people who wanted a less rough sport. It was soon renamed volleyball and spread through YMCAs around the world."]],
k:[["Invented","1895, by William G. Morgan"],["Players per side","6"],["Touches per side","Up to 3"],["Olympic sport since","1964 (beach volleyball since 1996)"],["World governing body","FIVB, founded 1947"]],
d:[["1895","William G. Morgan invents volleyball"],["1916","Players in the Philippines are credited with developing the set and spike"],["1947","The international federation FIVB is founded"],["1964","Volleyball debuts at the Tokyo Olympics"],["2025","Italy wins the men’s world championship"]],
s2:[["Inventing the spike","Volleyball reached Asia through the YMCA in the early 1900s. According to volleyball histories, around 1916 players in the Philippines began passing the ball high and then hitting it hard downward over the net, a technique called the bomba. This set and spike became a basic part of the game worldwide. The six-player game and rotation rules were also settled in this period."],["Beach volleyball","Beach volleyball, played on sand by teams of two, began on the beaches of California and became an Olympic sport in 1996. Players must cover the whole court with only one partner, making it demanding. Brazil and the United States have won the most Olympic beach volleyball medals."],["The volleyball craze","Volleyball draws huge crowds in many countries. Italy’s Serie A and Poland’s PlusLiga are among the strongest men’s leagues, Türkiye’s women’s clubs attract stars from around the world, and in Brazil and the Philippines volleyball matches fill arenas. Players such as Zhu Ting of China, Wilfredo León of Poland and Paola Egonu of Italy have huge followings."],["World stages","Volleyball has been an Olympic sport since 1964, when Japan’s women’s team, nicknamed the Witches of the Orient, won gold. Brazil, the United States, Italy, Poland, Serbia, China and Japan are among the strongest nations. World championships are held every few years in different host countries."],["Sitting volleyball","Sitting volleyball, played with a lower net by athletes with disabilities who must keep part of their body on the floor, is a Paralympic sport. It shows how the game can be adapted so more people can play."],["Why people love it","Volleyball can be played almost anywhere with a ball and a net, from village courts to beaches and gyms. It is fast, exciting and requires teamwork more than size alone. Rallies can last many hits, and dramatic comebacks are common because there is no game clock."]],
sp:["The Witches of the Orient","Japan’s women’s team of the early 1960s, made up mostly of factory workers from a textile company in Osaka, trained for hours every day under their demanding coach, Hirobumi Daimatsu. Nicknamed the Witches of the Orient, they won 258 matches in a row and took gold at the 1964 Tokyo Olympics, beating the Soviet Union in a final watched by most of Japan."],
g:[["Spike","A powerful downward attacking hit"],["Set","A pass that places the ball for a teammate to attack"],["Libero","A defensive specialist wearing a different color"],["Rally","An exchange of hits before a point is won"]],
f:["Volleyball was first called mintonette.","Players in the Philippines are credited with developing the spike.","Japan’s women won volleyball gold at the 1964 Tokyo Olympics."],
fun:"Volleyball was originally called mintonette when it was invented in 1895.",
tl:"Ang volleyball ay larong pangkoponan na may anim na manlalaro bawat panig na nagpapalipad ng bola sa ibabaw ng net. Naimbento ito noong 1895, at kinikilala ang mga Pilipino sa pagbuo ng spike; napakasikat nito sa Pilipinas."});

;
/* data/gaps7.js */
// Gap articles, part 7: Technology (smartphones, social media, cars, ships and boats, bridges).
ADDART({id:"smartphone",t:"Smartphones",c:"Technology",i:"📱",w:"Smartphone",r:["phone","internet","socialmedia"],
p:[["A computer in your pocket","A smartphone is a mobile phone that works like a small computer, with a touchscreen, apps and internet access."],["Everywhere","Billions of people use smartphones to talk, message, take photos, learn, shop and pay."],["Texting nation","In many countries, most people now get online through their phones rather than computers."]],
l:"A smartphone is a handheld device that combines a mobile phone with a computer, camera, music player, map and much more. Since the late 2000s, smartphones have changed how people communicate, work, learn, shop and entertain themselves, and for billions of people they are now the main way to use the internet.",
s:[["What is inside","A smartphone contains a processor, memory, storage, a battery, a touchscreen, cameras, microphones, speakers and antennas for mobile networks, Wi-Fi and Bluetooth. Sensors include an accelerometer that detects movement, a gyroscope, a compass, GPS for location and often fingerprint or face recognition for security."],["Apps","Smartphones run apps, programs downloaded from app stores, for almost any purpose: messaging, social media, maps, banking, games, learning and health. Apple’s App Store and Google Play both opened in 2008 and now offer millions of apps. People use apps to send money, call taxis and order food."],["The first smartphones","IBM’s Simon, sold in 1994, combined a phone with a touchscreen, email and calendar. In the 2000s, BlackBerry phones with small keyboards became popular for email. In 2007 Apple introduced the iPhone, with a large multi-touch screen, and in 2008 the first Android phone was released. Smartphones then spread rapidly worldwide."],["Mobile networks","Smartphones connect through mobile networks of cell towers. Each generation has brought faster speeds: 3G allowed basic internet, 4G made video streaming easy, and 5G, introduced around 2019, offers even faster connections with less delay. Signals can still be weak in remote mountains, deserts and islands."]],
k:[["First smartphone","IBM Simon, 1994"],["iPhone introduced","2007"],["First Android phone","2008"],["Main operating systems","Android and iOS"],["Smartphone users","More than 4 billion"]],
d:[["1994","IBM Simon, an early smartphone, goes on sale"],["2007","Apple introduces the iPhone"],["2008","App stores open and the first Android phone is released"],["2010s","Smartphones become the main way most people go online"],["2019","5G networks begin service in South Korea, the United States and other countries"]],
s2:[["The texting capital","Before smartphones, text messages were the favorite way for young people to communicate. Cheap texts made SMS hugely popular in the early 2000s, especially in Asia and Europe, where billions of messages were sent every day. Texts even helped gather crowds for protests, as in Manila in 2001."],["Mobile money","Smartphone apps have made it easy to send and receive money, pay bills and buy goods without cash. Mobile money services such as M-Pesa in Kenya, Alipay and WeChat Pay in China and UPI in India are used by hundreds of millions of people, including many without bank accounts. Migrant workers can send money home instantly."],["Cameras for everyone","Most smartphones have excellent cameras, so almost anyone can take photos and videos and share them instantly. This has changed journalism, as ordinary people record news events, and created new careers for content creators. It also raises questions about privacy and respect for others."],["Screen time","Using smartphones too much can affect sleep, concentration, physical activity and mood. Doctors suggest putting phones away at mealtimes and before bed, taking breaks and being mindful about time on social media and games. Many phones have tools to track and limit screen time."],["Phones and the planet","Making smartphones uses metals such as cobalt, lithium and gold, mined in ways that can harm people and the environment. Old phones become electronic waste. Using phones longer, repairing them and recycling them properly through official collection points help reduce the damage."],["Phones in disasters","Smartphones help keep people safe during disasters. Emergency alerts warn of storms, earthquakes and tsunamis, and people use apps to check weather forecasts, contact family and ask for help. After disasters, mobile networks are among the first services restored."]],
sp:["Mobile money","In 2007 the Kenyan mobile company Safaricom launched M-Pesa, which let people store and send money by text message from even the simplest phone. Within a few years most Kenyan adults used it, paying school fees, wages and shop bills without a bank account. A study found it helped lift about 2 percent of Kenyan households out of extreme poverty. Mobile money has since spread across Africa and Asia."],
g:[["App","A program downloaded to a phone or tablet"],["Operating system","The main software that runs a device"],["SMS","Short Message Service, sending text messages"],["E-wallet","An app for storing and sending money"]],
f:["The first smartphone, IBM Simon, went on sale in 1994.","Kenya’s M-Pesa, launched in 2007, was one of the first mobile money services.","The first text message, Merry Christmas, was sent in 1992."],
fun:"The Philippines was once known as the texting capital of the world.",
tl:"Ang smartphone ay teleponong parang maliit na computer, may touchscreen, app at internet. Dati ay tinawag ang Pilipinas na texting capital of the world, at ngayon ay sa telepono kumokonekta sa internet ang karamihan ng Pilipino."});

ADDART({id:"socialmedia",t:"Social Media",c:"Technology",i:"💬",w:"Social media",r:["internet","smartphone","democ"],
p:[["Connecting online","Social media are websites and apps where people share posts, photos, videos and messages."],["Billions of users","About five billion people use social media worldwide."],["Billions connected","More than 5 billion people, about six in ten of the world’s population, use social media."]],
l:"Social media are online platforms where people create and share content, connect with friends and communities and follow news and entertainment. Since the early 2000s, sites such as Friendster, Facebook, YouTube, Instagram and TikTok have changed how people communicate, do business and take part in politics. Social media bring people together but also raise concerns about misinformation, privacy, bullying and mental health.",
s:[["Early networks","Early social networks included Six Degrees in 1997, Friendster in 2002, which became hugely popular in Southeast Asia, and MySpace in 2003. Facebook began in 2004 at Harvard University and opened to everyone in 2006. YouTube launched in 2005, Twitter, now called X, in 2006, and Instagram in 2010."],["Video takes over","Short videos became the most popular form of content in the late 2010s, led by TikTok, launched internationally in 2017, along with Instagram Reels and YouTube Shorts. Live streaming lets people broadcast in real time. Millions of people now earn money as content creators, vloggers and online sellers."],["How feeds work","Social media apps use algorithms, computer programs that decide what each person sees in their feed, based on what they have liked, watched and shared. Algorithms keep users engaged but can also show people more and more of the same views, creating echo chambers, or promote shocking content that attracts attention."],["Social media around the world","Different places use different platforms. Facebook, YouTube, WhatsApp and Instagram are popular in most countries, but China has its own, such as WeChat, Weibo and Douyin, and Russia uses VKontakte. People in many countries spend more than three hours a day on social media. It is used for news, selling goods, staying in touch with family abroad and organizing help after disasters."]],
k:[["Users worldwide","About 5 billion"],["Facebook founded","2004"],["YouTube launched","2005"],["TikTok launched internationally","2017"],["Data protection law","EU General Data Protection Regulation, 2018"]],
d:[["1997","Six Degrees, an early social network, launches"],["2002","Friendster launches"],["2004","Facebook is founded"],["2012","Facebook reaches one billion users"],["2017","TikTok launches internationally"]],
s2:[["Misinformation","False information can spread faster than the truth on social media, especially during elections, health crises and disasters. Fake accounts and paid trolls can make false claims seem popular. Before sharing, it helps to check the source, look for reports from reliable news outlets and fact-checkers, and think about whether a post is designed to make people angry."],["Privacy","Social media companies collect large amounts of data about users to target advertising. Posts, photos and locations can be seen by strangers if privacy settings are not used. Laws such as the European Union’s General Data Protection Regulation of 2018 protect personal information, and users should think carefully about what they share."],["Cyberbullying","Cyberbullying, harassing or shaming someone online, can be very harmful. Many countries require schools to address bullying, including online. Anyone being bullied should keep evidence, block the bully and tell a trusted adult, teacher or the platform."],["Social media and wellbeing","Social media can help people feel connected, but too much use may affect sleep and mood, and constantly comparing oneself with others can lower self-esteem. Taking breaks, following accounts that make you feel good and spending time with friends offline can help keep a healthy balance."],["Power to organize","Social media help people organize causes, raise money for those in need, find missing people and coordinate relief after typhoons. They have also played big roles in elections and protests around the world. Governments and companies debate how to keep platforms open while limiting harmful content."],["Being a good digital citizen","Good digital citizens treat others with respect online, think before posting, protect their personal information, check facts before sharing and give credit to creators. Many platforms require users to be at least 13 years old. Parents and teachers can help young people learn to use social media wisely."]],
sp:["The rise and fall of Friendster","Friendster, launched in 2002 in the United States, was one of the first big social networks. It struggled with slow servers and lost its American users to MySpace and Facebook, but it became a sensation in Southeast Asia, where millions decorated their profiles and wrote testimonials for friends. By around 2009 Facebook had taken over, and Friendster closed its social network in 2011, a lesson in how quickly online fashions change."],
g:[["Algorithm","A set of computer instructions that decides what content to show"],["Content creator","A person who makes videos, posts or other content online"],["Misinformation","False or misleading information"],["Cyberbullying","Harassing or hurting someone online"]],
f:["About five billion people use social media.","Friendster was one of the first big social networks, launched in 2002.","Many apps require users to be at least 13."],
fun:"At one time, the Philippines had more Friendster users than any other country.",
tl:"Ang social media ay mga website at app kung saan nagbabahagi ang mga tao ng post, larawan at video. Kabilang ang mga Pilipino sa pinakaaktibong gumagamit nito, kaya mahalagang suriin ang balita bago magbahagi at igalang ang kapwa online."});

ADDART({id:"cars",t:"Cars",c:"Technology",i:"🚗",w:"Car",r:["jeepney","ev","steam"],
p:[["Horseless carriages","The first practical cars with gasoline engines were built in Germany in the 1880s."],["Cars for everyone","Henry Ford’s Model T and the assembly line made cars affordable in the early 1900s."],["The road ahead","Electric and self-driving cars are changing how people travel."]],
l:"Cars, or automobiles, are wheeled vehicles that carry people on roads, powered by engines or electric motors. Since Karl Benz built his motorwagen in 1885, cars have transformed cities, economies and daily life, giving people new freedom to travel. They have also brought traffic, pollution and road accidents. Today the world has more than a billion cars, and the industry is shifting toward electric vehicles.",
s:[["The first cars","In 1885 the German engineer Karl Benz built a three-wheeled vehicle powered by a gasoline engine, patented in January 1886, often called the first true automobile. In 1888 his wife, Bertha Benz, drove it about 100 kilometers to show it worked. Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach built other early cars in Germany."],["The Model T","In 1908 Henry Ford introduced the Model T, a simple, tough car. In 1913 his factory began using a moving assembly line, cutting the time to build a car from about 12 hours to about 90 minutes. Prices fell so much that ordinary families could afford cars. More than 15 million Model Ts were built."],["How a car works","Most cars have an internal combustion engine that burns gasoline or diesel inside cylinders, pushing pistons that turn a crankshaft and, through the gearbox, the wheels. Electric cars use batteries to power electric motors instead. Brakes, steering, suspension and tires keep the car under control."],["Safety","Cars have become much safer thanks to seat belts, airbags, crumple zones that absorb impacts and anti-lock brakes. The modern three-point seat belt, invented by Nils Bohlin at Volvo in 1959, has saved millions of lives. Most countries now require drivers and passengers to wear seat belts."]],
k:[["First practical car","Benz Patent-Motorwagen, 1885–86"],["Model T introduced","1908"],["Moving assembly line","1913"],["Cars in the world","More than 1 billion"],["Three-point seat belt","1959, Nils Bohlin"]],
d:[["1886","Karl Benz patents his motorwagen"],["1888","Bertha Benz makes the first long-distance car journey"],["1908","Ford introduces the Model T"],["1913","Ford begins the moving assembly line"],["1959","Volvo introduces the three-point seat belt"]],
s2:[["Cars change the world","Cars allowed people to live farther from where they worked, leading to the growth of suburbs, highways, gas stations, shopping malls and drive-through restaurants. The car industry became one of the world’s largest employers. Cars also became symbols of freedom and status."],["Cars around the world","There are about 1.5 billion cars in the world. China became the largest car market in 2009, and the United States has one of the highest numbers of cars per person. In many cities, though, most people travel by bus, train, motorcycle or shared minibus, such as Kenya’s matatus, the Philippines’ jeepneys and Thailand’s tuk-tuks, and traffic jams are a daily challenge from Lagos to Jakarta."],["Pollution and climate","Gasoline and diesel engines release exhaust gases that pollute city air and harm health, as well as carbon dioxide that contributes to climate change. Laws such as the United States Clean Air Act of 1970 and European emission standards forced cleaner engines. Cleaner fuels, public transport and electric vehicles help reduce pollution."],["Electric cars","Electric cars run on rechargeable batteries and produce no exhaust. Early electric cars existed in the 1800s, but gasoline cars won out. Since the 2010s, better batteries have made electric cars popular in many countries. In Norway nearly nine out of ten new cars sold in 2024 were electric, and China makes more electric cars than any other country."],["Road safety","Around 1.2 million people die in road crashes worldwide each year. Many deaths could be prevented by wearing seat belts and helmets, obeying speed limits, not driving after drinking alcohol and not using phones while driving. Pedestrians, cyclists and motorcycle riders are especially at risk, and most deaths occur in low- and middle-income countries."],["Self-driving cars","Companies are developing cars that can drive themselves using cameras, radar, lidar sensors and artificial intelligence. Some robotaxis already carry passengers in a few cities. Self-driving cars could make roads safer and help people who cannot drive, but they raise questions about safety, jobs and responsibility in accidents."]],
sp:["Bertha Benz’s road trip","In August 1888, without telling her husband, Bertha Benz took her two teenage sons on a drive of about 100 kilometers from Mannheim to Pforzheim in Germany, in Karl Benz’s motorwagen. She bought fuel from a pharmacy, fixed a blocked fuel line with a hatpin and had a shoemaker replace brake linings. Her journey proved the car was practical."],
g:[["Internal combustion engine","An engine that burns fuel inside cylinders"],["Assembly line","A system in which products move past workers who each add a part"],["Emissions","Gases released by engines"],["Crumple zone","A part of a car designed to crush and absorb crash energy"]],
f:["Bertha Benz made the first long-distance car trip in 1888.","More than 15 million Ford Model Ts were built.","The three-point seat belt was invented in 1959."],
fun:"On the first long-distance car journey in 1888, Bertha Benz bought fuel at a pharmacy.",
tl:"Ang kotse ay sasakyang de-gulong na pinapatakbo ng makina o de-kuryenteng motor. Naimbento ito sa Germany noong 1880s, pinasikat ng Model T ni Henry Ford, at ngayon ay papunta na sa mga de-kuryenteng sasakyan."});

ADDART({id:"ships",t:"Ships and Boats",c:"Technology",i:"🚢",w:"Ship",r:["precol","explor","ofw"],
p:[["Floating on water","Boats and ships have carried people and goods across water for tens of thousands of years."],["Island nation","For island and coastal peoples, from Polynesia to Norway, boats have always been essential."],["World trade","Most of the world’s trade is carried by ship."]],
l:"Boats and ships are vessels that float and travel on water, carrying people and cargo across rivers, lakes and oceans. From dugout canoes and Austronesian outriggers to sailing galleons, steamships and giant container vessels, they have made exploration, trade and migration possible, and they still carry most of the world’s goods.",
s:[["Why ships float","A ship floats because it pushes aside, or displaces, a weight of water equal to its own weight, a principle discovered by the Greek scientist Archimedes. A steel ship floats because its hollow shape displaces a great deal of water. If water gets inside and the ship becomes heavier than the water it displaces, it sinks."],["Early boats","The earliest boats were probably rafts, dugout canoes carved from logs and boats of reeds or animal skins. People reached Australia by sea at least 50,000 years ago. Austronesian sailors from Taiwan and island Southeast Asia developed outrigger canoes and sailed across the Pacific and Indian oceans, reaching Madagascar, Hawaii and New Zealand."],["The age of sail","Sailing ships carried explorers, traders and settlers across the oceans. Chinese junks with watertight compartments, Arab dhows, Viking longships and European caravels and galleons each suited their seas. The magnetic compass, first used for navigation in China, helped sailors find their way."],["Steam and steel","In the 1800s, steam engines and steel hulls replaced sails and wood. Steamships could travel on schedule regardless of wind. The Suez Canal, opened in 1869, and the Panama Canal, opened in 1914, shortened voyages around the world. By the early 1900s, huge ocean liners such as the Titanic carried thousands of passengers."]],
k:[["Floating principle","Archimedes’ principle"],["Share of world trade by sea","About 80 percent"],["First container ship voyage","1956"],["Largest container ships","About 400 m long, carrying more than 24,000 containers"],["Deadliest peacetime sea disaster","MV Doña Paz, 1987"]],
d:[["c. 2500 BCE","The Khufu ship is built in Egypt"],["1492","Columbus crosses the Atlantic"],["1807","Robert Fulton’s steamboat begins regular service in New York"],["1956","The first container ship sails from New Jersey"],["1987","The MV Doña Paz sinks after a collision in the Tablas Strait"]],
s2:[["The banca","Many traditional boats are still in use. Arab dhows with triangular sails trade along the coasts of the Indian Ocean, Chinese junks once crossed whole oceans, and outrigger boats such as the Filipino banca and the Sri Lankan oruwa keep steady in the waves with floats on bamboo poles. Reed boats are still built on Lake Titicaca in the Andes."],["Ocean trade routes","From 1565 to 1815, Spanish galleons sailed between Manila and Acapulco in Mexico, carrying Chinese silk and porcelain east and Mexican silver west, the first regular route across the Pacific. In the Indian Ocean, monsoon winds carried dhows between Arabia, India and East Africa, and Dutch and English East India Company ships sailed around Africa to Asia."],["Container ships","In 1956 the American businessman Malcolm McLean sent the first container ship, the Ideal X, from New Jersey to Texas. Packing goods into standard steel boxes made loading much faster and cheaper, transforming world trade. The largest container ships today carry more than 20,000 containers and are about 400 meters long."],["Ferries and the nautical highway","Ferries and roll-on, roll-off ships carry passengers, cars and trucks across straits and between islands. In places such as Greece, Indonesia, Norway and the Philippines, ferries are part of daily life, and long-distance ferries link Britain to continental Europe and Japan’s islands to each other."],["Safety at sea","Sea travel can be dangerous. The Titanic sank in 1912 with the loss of about 1,500 lives, leading to the first international rules on lifeboats and radio watch. On 20 December 1987, the overcrowded ferry MV Doña Paz collided with an oil tanker in the Philippines and caught fire; more than 4,000 people died, the deadliest peacetime maritime disaster in history. Today coast guards stop ships from sailing in dangerous storms."],["Seafarers","About 1.9 million seafarers crew the world’s merchant ships, working for months at a time far from home. Many come from the Philippines, Indonesia, China, India and Russia. Their work keeps world trade moving, and the Day of the Seafarer honors them every 25 June."]],
sp:["Hōkūleʻa","In 1976 the Hōkūleʻa, a replica of an ancient Polynesian double-hulled voyaging canoe, sailed from Hawaii to Tahiti using only traditional navigation: the stars, the swells, the winds and the flight of birds. Guided by the master navigator Mau Piailug of Micronesia, the voyage proved that Polynesians could have settled the Pacific on purpose. From 2014 to 2017 Hōkūleʻa sailed around the world."],
g:[["Hull","The main body of a ship"],["Outrigger","A float attached to the side of a boat for stability"],["Displacement","The weight of water pushed aside by a floating object"],["Container ship","A ship that carries goods in standard steel boxes"]],
f:["About 80 percent of world trade travels by sea.","About 1.9 million seafarers work on the world’s merchant ships.","The first container ship sailed in 1956."],
fun:"Many of the Spanish Manila galleons were built by Filipino shipbuilders in Cavite.",
tl:"Ang mga bangka at barko ay nagdadala ng tao at kalakal sa tubig sa loob ng libu-libong taon. Para sa kapuluang Pilipinas, mahalaga ang mga ito, mula sa balangay at bangka hanggang sa mga barkong pangkargamento."});

ADDART({id:"bridges",t:"Bridges",c:"Technology",i:"🌉",w:"Bridge",r:["rome","rail","cars"],
p:[["Crossing gaps","Bridges carry roads, railways and paths over rivers, valleys and seas."],["Many designs","Beam, arch, truss, suspension and cable-stayed bridges each handle forces in different ways."],["Linking islands","Long bridges join islands to mainlands and to each other, such as Denmark’s Great Belt bridges and the Hong Kong–Zhuhai–Macau Bridge."]],
l:"A bridge is a structure that carries a road, railway or path across an obstacle such as a river, valley, road or stretch of sea. From simple logs laid across streams to steel suspension bridges kilometers long, bridges are among humanity’s greatest engineering achievements. Engineers choose designs based on the distance to cross, the loads to carry and forces such as wind, earthquakes and floods.",
s:[["Forces on a bridge","Every bridge must handle two main forces: compression, which squeezes materials, and tension, which stretches them. Its own weight, the traffic it carries, wind and earthquakes all push and pull on it. Good bridge designs spread these forces safely to the ground through supports called piers and abutments."],["Beam and arch bridges","The simplest bridge is a beam resting on supports at each end, like a plank over a stream. Arch bridges, used since ancient times, push their load outward and down into strong supports at each end. The Romans built stone arch bridges and aqueducts, some still standing after 2,000 years."],["Truss and cantilever bridges","Truss bridges use frameworks of triangles, which are very strong shapes, to support loads with less material. Cantilever bridges have arms that stick out from piers and meet in the middle. Many railway bridges of the 1800s and 1900s used these designs."],["Suspension and cable-stayed bridges","Suspension bridges hang the deck from vertical cables attached to huge main cables slung between tall towers, allowing very long spans. Cable-stayed bridges connect the deck directly to towers with straight cables in fan or harp patterns. Both are used to cross wide rivers and straits."]],
k:[["Longest suspension span","1915 Çanakkale Bridge, Türkiye, 2,023 m"],["Longest bridge overall","Danyang–Kunshan Grand Bridge, China, about 164 km"],["Longest sea crossing","Hong Kong–Zhuhai–Macau Bridge, about 55 km"],["Main forces","Compression and tension"],["Main types","Beam, arch, truss, suspension, cable-stayed"]],
d:[["1779","The Iron Bridge in England becomes the first major cast-iron bridge"],["1883","The Brooklyn Bridge opens in New York"],["1937","The Golden Gate Bridge opens in San Francisco"],["1998","Japan’s Akashi Kaikyō Bridge opens with the world’s longest suspension span"],["2018","The Hong Kong–Zhuhai–Macau Bridge opens"]],
s2:[["Famous bridges","The Brooklyn Bridge of 1883 was the first steel-wire suspension bridge and the longest of its time. San Francisco’s Golden Gate Bridge, opened in 1937, is famous for its orange color. London’s Tower Bridge lifts to let ships pass. Sydney Harbour Bridge, a giant steel arch, opened in 1932."],["Record breakers","The 1915 Çanakkale Bridge in Türkiye, opened in 2022, has the longest main suspension span in the world at 2,023 meters. The longest bridge of any kind is the Danyang–Kunshan Grand Bridge in China, a railway viaduct about 164 kilometers long. China has built many of the world’s longest and highest bridges in recent decades."],["Bridges that link islands","Some of the world’s most ambitious bridges join islands. Japan’s Akashi Kaikyō Bridge links Honshu to Awaji Island. The Øresund Bridge and tunnel, opened in 2000, connects Denmark and Sweden, and the Hong Kong–Zhuhai–Macau Bridge, opened in 2018, crosses about 55 kilometers of sea with bridges, islands and a tunnel. The San Juanico Bridge joins the islands of Samar and Leyte in the Philippines."],["Bridges of great cities","Many cities are famous for their bridges. Paris has 37 bridges across the Seine, St. Petersburg raises its bridges at night to let ships through, and Venice’s Rialto Bridge has crossed the Grand Canal since 1591. London’s Tower Bridge, opened in 1894, lifts for tall ships, and Sydney Harbour Bridge, opened in 1932, is nicknamed the Coathanger."],["When bridges fail","In 1940 the Tacoma Narrows Bridge in the United States twisted and collapsed in strong wind, only months after opening, because its deck caught the wind like a wing. The disaster taught engineers to test bridge designs for wind. Bridges must also be inspected and maintained to prevent failures from rust, overloading and earthquakes."],["Bridges for the future","Engineers are planning ever longer bridges, such as the proposed bridge across the Strait of Messina between Sicily and mainland Italy, which would have the longest suspension span ever. New bridges are designed to withstand earthquakes, typhoons and rising seas, and sensors monitor their health."]],
sp:["Galloping Gertie","The first Tacoma Narrows Bridge in Washington State was so flexible that its deck rose and fell in waves even in light winds, earning it the nickname Galloping Gertie. On 7 November 1940, in a wind of about 64 kilometers per hour, it began twisting violently and collapsed into the water. No people died, though a dog trapped in a car was lost. Film of the collapse is still shown to engineering students."],
g:[["Span","The distance between two supports of a bridge"],["Compression","A squeezing force"],["Tension","A stretching force"],["Pier","A support that holds up a bridge"]],
f:["The Hong Kong–Zhuhai–Macau Bridge crosses about 55 kilometers of sea.","The longest bridge in the world is about 164 km long.","The Tacoma Narrows Bridge collapsed in the wind in 1940."],
fun:"The longest bridge in the world, in China, is about 164 kilometers long.",
tl:"Ang tulay ay estrukturang nagdadala ng daan o riles sa ibabaw ng ilog, lambak o dagat. May iba’t ibang uri nito gaya ng arch at suspension, at ang San Juanico Bridge ang pinakamahabang tulay sa ibabaw ng tubig sa Pilipinas."});

;
/* data/gaps8.js */
// Gap articles, part 8: The Human Body and life science (the nervous system, mental health, the human genome).
ADDART({id:"nervous",t:"The Nervous System",c:"Health and the Body",i:"⚡",w:"Nervous system",r:["brain","spine","memory"],
p:[["The body’s messenger","The nervous system carries signals between the brain and every part of the body."],["Two main parts","The brain and spinal cord form the central nervous system; nerves throughout the body form the peripheral system."],["Lightning fast","Some nerve signals travel at more than 400 kilometers per hour."]],
l:"The nervous system is the body’s communication network. It collects information from the senses, processes it in the brain and spinal cord, and sends commands to muscles and organs, all in fractions of a second. It controls movement, thought and feeling as well as automatic actions such as breathing and heartbeat. It is made of billions of nerve cells called neurons.",
s:[["Central and peripheral","The central nervous system is made up of the brain and the spinal cord, protected by the skull and the backbone. The peripheral nervous system is the network of nerves branching from them to the skin, muscles and organs. There are 12 pairs of cranial nerves from the brain and 31 pairs of spinal nerves from the spinal cord."],["Neurons","Neurons are cells specialized to carry electrical signals. Each has branches called dendrites that receive signals, a cell body and a long fiber called an axon that sends signals on. Many axons are wrapped in a fatty layer called myelin, which speeds up signals. Some axons, from the spine to the toes, are about a meter long."],["Synapses","Neurons do not touch. Signals cross tiny gaps called synapses using chemical messengers called neurotransmitters, such as dopamine and serotonin. Each neuron can connect to thousands of others, creating a network of enormous complexity. Many medicines work by changing how neurotransmitters act."],["Reflexes","Some responses happen without thinking. When you touch something hot, sensory nerves send a signal to the spinal cord, which immediately signals your arm muscles to pull away, before the brain even feels pain. Doctors test reflexes, such as the knee jerk, to check that nerves are working."]],
k:[["Main parts","Brain, spinal cord and nerves"],["Neurons in the brain","About 86 billion"],["Fastest signals","More than 100 m/s"],["Cranial nerves","12 pairs"],["Spinal nerves","31 pairs"]],
d:[["c. 1600 BCE","An Egyptian papyrus describes injuries to the brain and spine"],["1791","Luigi Galvani shows that nerves carry electricity"],["1906","Camillo Golgi and Santiago Ramón y Cajal share a Nobel Prize for studying neurons"],["1921","Otto Loewi shows that nerves signal with chemicals"],["1963","A Nobel Prize is awarded for explaining how nerve signals travel"]],
s2:[["Automatic control","The autonomic nervous system controls things we do not think about, such as heartbeat, breathing, digestion and sweating. Its sympathetic branch prepares the body for action in the fight-or-flight response, speeding the heart and widening the pupils. Its parasympathetic branch calms the body for rest and digestion."],["The spinal cord","The spinal cord, about 45 centimeters long in adults, runs down a channel inside the backbone. It carries messages between the brain and body and handles reflexes. Damage to the spinal cord can cause paralysis below the injury, which is why protecting the neck and back in accidents and sports is so important."],["The senses","Sensory receptors in the eyes, ears, nose, tongue and skin turn light, sound, chemicals, pressure and temperature into nerve signals. The brain interprets these signals to create our experience of the world. Some receptors tell the brain where the body’s parts are, so we can touch our nose with our eyes closed."],["Disorders","Nervous system disorders include stroke, epilepsy, which causes seizures, Parkinson’s disease, which affects movement, multiple sclerosis, which damages myelin, and dementia. Infections such as meningitis can also affect it. Many disorders can be treated or managed with medicines, surgery and therapy."],["Repair","Nerves in the peripheral nervous system can slowly regrow after injury, about a millimeter a day. Damage to the brain and spinal cord heals poorly, but the brain can sometimes rewire itself so other areas take over lost functions. Scientists are researching ways to repair spinal cord injuries."],["Looking after nerves","Wearing helmets and seat belts protects the brain and spine. A healthy diet with B vitamins, regular exercise and enough sleep keep the nervous system working well. Diabetes can damage nerves, so controlling blood sugar matters. Avoiding alcohol, tobacco and drugs protects the brain, especially while it is still developing."]],
sp:["Cajal’s drawings","The Spanish scientist Santiago Ramón y Cajal spent years looking at stained brain tissue under a microscope and drew neurons in beautiful, detailed pictures. He showed that the nervous system is made of separate cells that communicate across gaps, not one continuous web. He shared the 1906 Nobel Prize and is often called the father of modern neuroscience. His drawings are still used in textbooks."],
g:[["Neuron","A nerve cell that carries signals"],["Synapse","A gap where one neuron passes a signal to another"],["Reflex","An automatic, rapid response"],["Myelin","A fatty layer that speeds nerve signals"]],
f:["Some nerve signals travel faster than 100 meters per second.","Some axons are about a meter long.","Reflexes can act before the brain feels pain."],
fun:"A nerve signal can travel from your toe to your brain in about a hundredth of a second.",
tl:"Ang nervous system ang sistemang pangkomunikasyon ng katawan, na binubuo ng utak, spinal cord at mga nerbiyo. Kinokontrol nito ang galaw, pag-iisip at mga awtomatikong gawain gaya ng paghinga at tibok ng puso."});

ADDART({id:"mental",t:"Mental Health",c:"Health and the Body",i:"💚",w:"Mental health",r:["brain","sleep","exercise"],
p:[["Health of the mind","Mental health is about how we think, feel and act, and how we cope with life’s challenges."],["Common and treatable","About one in eight people worldwide lives with a mental health condition, and most can be helped."],["It’s okay to ask for help","Talking to someone you trust is an important first step."]],
l:"Mental health is our emotional, psychological and social wellbeing. It affects how we think, feel, act, relate to others and handle stress. Everyone has mental health, just as everyone has physical health, and it can change over time. Mental health conditions such as anxiety and depression are common and treatable, and seeking help is a sign of strength.",
s:[["What mental health means","Good mental health does not mean feeling happy all the time. It means being able to cope with normal stresses, work and learn, enjoy relationships and contribute to one’s community. Everyone feels sad, worried or angry sometimes. These feelings become a concern when they are very strong, last a long time or make daily life hard."],["Common conditions","Anxiety disorders, with constant worry, fear or panic, and depression, with lasting sadness, loss of interest and tiredness, are the most common conditions. Others include bipolar disorder, eating disorders, attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder and schizophrenia. The World Health Organization estimates that about one in eight people worldwide lives with a mental disorder."],["What affects mental health","Mental health is shaped by many things, including genes, brain chemistry, physical health, life experiences, stress, trauma, loneliness, poverty and discrimination. Support from family and friends, a safe environment, healthy habits and access to care help protect it."],["Getting help","Mental health conditions can be treated with talking therapies, called psychotherapy or counseling, medicines and support from family and community. The earlier someone gets help, the better. Speaking with a parent, teacher, guidance counselor, doctor or other trusted adult is a good first step."]],
k:[["People affected worldwide","About 1 in 8"],["Most common conditions","Anxiety and depression"],["Global plan","WHO Mental Health Action Plan, 2013 to 2030"],["People with depression","About 280 million worldwide"],["World Mental Health Day","10 October"]],
d:[["1928","The Insular Psychopathic Hospital, forerunner of the National Center for Mental Health, opens"],["1992","World Mental Health Day is first observed"],["2013","The WHO adopts its first Mental Health Action Plan"],["2022","The 988 crisis line opens in the United States"],["2019","The WHO recognizes burnout as an occupational phenomenon"]],
s2:[["Looking after your mind","Simple habits support mental health: getting enough sleep, eating regular meals, being physically active, spending time outdoors and with people you care about, limiting screen time and doing things you enjoy. Talking about feelings, writing them down and practicing slow breathing can help in stressful moments."],["Stress","Stress is the body’s response to challenges, such as exams, conflicts or big changes. A little stress can help us focus, but long-lasting stress can harm sleep, health and mood. Breaking tasks into small steps, taking breaks, asking for help and doing relaxing activities can reduce stress."],["Stigma","Many people with mental health conditions face stigma, negative attitudes that make them feel ashamed or afraid to seek help. Talking openly about mental health, using respectful language and listening without judging help reduce stigma. Mental health conditions are health conditions, not personal weaknesses."],["Mental health around the world","The World Health Organization estimates that about one in eight people in the world lives with a mental health condition, yet in many countries most of them receive no care. Mental health services are scarce in poorer countries, and stigma keeps many people from seeking help everywhere. The WHO’s Mental Health Action Plan 2013 to 2030 urges countries to treat mental health as part of everyday health care."],["Helping a friend","If a friend seems to be struggling, you can help by listening, telling them you care and encouraging them to talk to a trusted adult or professional. You do not have to fix their problems. If someone talks about wanting to hurt themselves, tell a trusted adult right away and help them contact a crisis hotline or emergency services."],["Where to find help","Talking to a trusted adult, a friend, a school counselor, a teacher or a doctor is a good first step. Many countries have free, confidential crisis lines open day and night, such as 988 in the United States and Canada, the Samaritans on 116 123 in the United Kingdom and Ireland and Lifeline on 13 11 14 in Australia. In an emergency, call your local emergency number."]],
sp:["Talking helps","Many people find that simply talking about how they feel makes a difference. Sharing worries with a trusted person can bring relief, new ideas and the reminder that you are not alone. Counselors, psychologists and psychiatrists are trained to help, and what you tell them is kept confidential except when someone’s safety is at risk."],
g:[["Mental health","Emotional, psychological and social wellbeing"],["Anxiety","Strong, lasting worry or fear"],["Depression","Lasting sadness and loss of interest that affect daily life"],["Stigma","Negative attitudes that cause shame or unfair treatment"]],
f:["About one in eight people worldwide lives with a mental health condition.","About one in eight people in the world lives with a mental health condition.","World Mental Health Day is 10 October."],
fun:"World Mental Health Day has been observed every 10 October since 1992.",
tl:"Ang kalusugang pangkaisipan ay tungkol sa ating damdamin, pag-iisip at pakikitungo sa iba. Karaniwan at nagagamot ang mga kondisyon gaya ng anxiety at depression; maaaring tumawag sa NCMH Crisis Hotline sa 1553 kapag kailangan ng tulong."});

ADDART({id:"genome",t:"The Human Genome",c:"Science",i:"🧬",w:"Human genome",r:["dna","mendel","evo"],
p:[["The instruction book","The human genome is the complete set of DNA instructions in a human cell."],["Three billion letters","It contains about 3.1 billion base pairs of DNA and around 20,000 genes that make proteins."],["Read in 2003","The Human Genome Project finished reading the human genome in 2003."]],
l:"The human genome is the complete set of genetic instructions found in almost every human cell, written in about 3.1 billion pairs of DNA bases. It contains around 20,000 genes that code for proteins, along with vast stretches of DNA that regulate genes or whose roles are still being studied. Reading it, completed by the Human Genome Project in 2003, transformed medicine and our understanding of human history.",
s:[["Genes and chromosomes","The human genome is packed into 23 pairs of chromosomes in the nucleus of each cell, one set from each parent, plus a small amount of DNA in the mitochondria. Genes are stretches of DNA that carry instructions for making proteins. Surprisingly, only about 1 to 2 percent of the genome codes for proteins."],["The Human Genome Project","Launched in 1990, the Human Genome Project was an international effort involving scientists in the United States, the United Kingdom, Japan, France, Germany and China. A private company, Celera Genomics, raced to read the genome too. A first draft was announced in 2000, and the project was declared complete in April 2003, at a cost of about 2.7 billion US dollars."],["Filling the gaps","The 2003 genome still had gaps in hard-to-read regions. In 2022 the Telomere-to-Telomere Consortium published the first truly complete human genome sequence, from one end of each chromosome to the other. Scientists are now building a pangenome, combining genomes from many people around the world, to capture human diversity."],["We are all alike","Any two people share about 99.9 percent of their DNA. The small differences contribute to variations in appearance, risk of some diseases and responses to medicines. Studies of genomes show that all humans are closely related and that there is more genetic variation within populations than between them."]],
k:[["Base pairs","About 3.1 billion"],["Protein-coding genes","About 20,000"],["Chromosomes","23 pairs"],["Human Genome Project","1990–2003"],["Shared between any two people","About 99.9 percent"]],
d:[["1953","The double-helix structure of DNA is described"],["1990","The Human Genome Project begins"],["2003","The Human Genome Project is completed"],["2018","England’s 100,000 Genomes Project completes its sequencing"],["2022","The first gap-free human genome sequence is published"]],
s2:[["Cheaper and faster","Reading the first human genome took 13 years and billions of dollars. New machines can now sequence a person’s genome in a day or two for a few hundred dollars. This has made it possible to study the genomes of millions of people and to use genetic tests in medicine."],["Genomes and medicine","Knowing the genome helps doctors diagnose rare genetic diseases, choose cancer treatments that target specific mutations and predict how patients will respond to some medicines, an approach called precision medicine. Genetic testing of newborns can catch treatable conditions early, and many countries screen all newborn babies."],["Genes and environment","Most traits and common diseases, such as height, diabetes and heart disease, are influenced by many genes together with environment and lifestyle. Having a gene variant linked to a disease does not mean a person will definitely get it. Healthy habits can greatly reduce risks."],["Our ancient past","Genomes reveal human history. They show that modern humans arose in Africa and spread around the world, and that many people carry small amounts of DNA from Neanderthals and Denisovans. Studies have found that the Ayta Magbukon of the Philippines carry one of the highest known proportions of Denisovan ancestry."],["Genomes for millions","After the first genome, countries began reading genomes on a huge scale. England’s 100,000 Genomes Project finished in 2018, the UK Biobank has data on half a million volunteers, and projects in the United States, Africa and Asia aim to include people of many ancestries. During the COVID-19 pandemic, labs worldwide sequenced virus samples to track new variants."],["Ethics and privacy","Genetic information is personal and can reveal information about relatives too. Laws in many countries protect people from discrimination based on their genes. Questions remain about who can access genetic data, how it is stored and whether genes should be edited, especially in embryos."]],
sp:["Denisovan ancestors","Denisovans were an ancient group of humans known mostly from DNA in a few bones found in Siberia and Tibet. A 2021 study found that the Ayta Magbukon, an Indigenous Negrito people of Bataan, carry more Denisovan DNA than any other group studied, suggesting their ancestors mixed with Denisovans who once lived in the islands of Southeast Asia."],
g:[["Genome","The complete set of an organism’s DNA"],["Gene","A section of DNA that carries instructions for a protein or function"],["Chromosome","A package of DNA in the cell nucleus"],["Sequencing","Reading the order of bases in DNA"]],
f:["The human genome has about 3.1 billion base pairs.","Only about 1–2 percent of the genome codes for proteins.","The Ayta Magbukon carry one of the highest levels of Denisovan DNA known."],
fun:"Any two people share about 99.9 percent of their DNA.",
tl:"Ang human genome ay ang buong hanay ng DNA sa selula ng tao, na may mga 3.1 bilyong pares ng base at mga 20,000 gene. Natapos itong basahin noong 2003, at nakatutulong ito sa medisina at sa pag-unawa sa kasaysayan ng tao."});

;
